    "ls|LS16_S9_U_317": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 317 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON.  19.07.2016 \n \nE-Waste Trade \n \n317. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to formulate a new legislation to control e -waste \ntrade in the country and if so, the details of the scheme formulated in this regard;  \n(b) whether the Government has constituted an expert committee to assess financial loss \nand adverse impact on human beings due to e-waste; \n(c) if so, the details and present status thereof; and  \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) e-Waste trade is regulated through the multiple provisions of e-Waste \n(Management) Rules, notified in March, 2016 and Hazardous and Other waste \n(Management and Transboundary Movement) Rules, notified in April 2016. Import \nof e-waste for disposal  in the country  is prohibited under these rules. Import of e -\nwaste for recycling is regulated through prior approval of the M inistry.  However, \nno permission has been granted by the Ministry for any such import of e -waste \nduring last five years. The procedure for import of used and refurbished electronic \nand electrical equipments for various purposes including  for Research & \nDevelopment, training, spare parts for warranty replacement, repair, renting and \nothers has been simplified, and in most of the cases it is based upon Standard \nOperating Procedure.  \n \nThe domestic trading of e -waste is regulated e -Waste (Management) Rules, 2016 \nwhich provides for extended producer r esponsibility making it obligatory for all the \nproducers to put in place mechanism for environmentally sound channelization of e-\nwaste to authorized recyclers/dismantlers. The Rules have the flexibility providing \nfor instrume nts like setting up of producer responsibility organizations, e-waste \nexchange, and financial mechanism in the form of Deposit Refund Scheme enabling \nproducers to collect and channelize e -waste, their dismantling for recycling \npurposes. S pecific responsibility has also been assigned to  bulk consumers of \nelectronic products for safe disposal  of end of life electronic and electrical \n\nequipments. Simplification in permission process for dismantling and recycling  \nfacilities through single authorization through Standard Operating Procedure  has \nbeen incorporated. \n(b) No expert committee has been  constituted by this Ministry  to assess financial loss \nand adverse impact on human beings due to e-waste. \n \n(c)&(d) Does not arise in view of answer at (b) above. \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_318": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.318 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA)  \n \nWOMEN  IN CAPF \n \n318. SHRI K. ASHOK KUMAR: \n \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has decided  to appoint woman officers in \nCentral Armed Police Forces (CAPF) and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government has  recently published new rules allowing  \nwomen to apply as direct entry officers in  the border g uarding force \nIndo-Tibetan Border Police; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether it is true that by allowing  women to apply as direct entry \nofficers in ITBP, all restrictions have been lifted on women; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a): Recruitment of officers on the post s of Assistant Commandant \n(General Duty), Specialist Medical Officer and General Duty Medical \nOfficer is open to all male/female candidates in BSF, ITBP, CRPF, SSB & \nCISF.  In Assam Rifles, women officers are appointed in Medical Cadre \nonly. \n \n(b) to (e) : Women have been allowed direct entry as Assistant \nCommandant(General Duty) in ITBP by amending the Indo-Tibetan Border \nPolice Force, General Duty Cadre(Group ‘A’ Posts), Recruitment Rules, \n2010  w.e.f. 11.05.2015. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_319": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION AND LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED  QUESTION NO.  319 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 8TH AUGUST, 2016 \n \nFunds for Right to Education \n \n*319. SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL:  \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE  DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n \n (a) the funds requested by the States/UT Governments for the purpose of implementation of \nRight to Education Act (RTE), 2009;  \n \n (b) whether the said funds were/are allocated/utilized by the respective State Governments and \nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n (c) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to ensure effective implementation of the \nsaid Act ? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n \n(a) to (c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 319 TO BE ANSWERED ON 8 TH AUGUST, 2016 ASKED BY \nSHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL REGARDING FUNDS FOR RIGHT TO EDUCATION \n \n \n(a): Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan (SSA) is the designat ed Centrally Sponsored Scheme for meeting the \nobjective of the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) Act, 2009. The details of \nfunds requested/proposed by the State Governments and UT Administrations for the SSA-RTE \nprogramme in the last two years and the current year are at Annexure I. \n \n(b): Allocation of outlays of the States and UT s under SSA is made on the basis of the approved \nAnnual Work Plan and Budgets (AWP&Bs) prepared by States based on their requirements and \npriority. Release of funds depends on timely submi ssion by States and UTs of the requisite documents \nsuch as utilisation certificate, progress report, a udit report, etc as per the scheme guidelines and \nGeneral Financial Rules. State/UT-wise details of central share released and expenditure reported by \nthem under SSA in the last two years and the current year are at Annexure-II. \n \n(c): Apart from the financial a ssistance provided to State Governments and UT Administrations, \nseveral initiatives have been taken up by the Central Government for effective implementation of the \nRTE Act, 2009 and SSA programme.  Some of these are:- \n(i) Since the inception of SSA in 2001 till 31.3.2016, 3.64 lakh new elementary schools,  \nconstruction of 3.11 lakh school buildings, and 18.61 lakh additional classrooms, 2.38 \nlakh drinking water facilities, 10.14 lakh school toilets and 19.48 lakh teacher posts \nhave been sanctioned to States and UTs. \n(ii) Kasturba Gandhi Balika Vidyalayas (KGBVs) have been opened in Educationally \nBackward Blocks (EBBs) where the female rural literacy is below the national average \nto provide for residential upper primary schools for girls. 3609 KGBVs have been \nsanctioned till 31.3.2016, out of which 3599 KGBVs are operational in the country \nenrolling 3.64 lakh girls.  \n(iii) Under Swachh Vidyalaya initiative, 4,17,796 toilets were constructed in 2,61,400 \nelementary and secondary government schools in a period of one year from 15.8.2014 \nto 15.8.2015, thus ensuring that every government school has separate functional toilets \nfor girls and boys. With this, about 13.58 crore children in 11.08 lakh government \nschools have access to gender segregated toilets.  \n(iv) The number of children with special needs that have been brought into the fold of \neducation (through enrolment in formal schools, school readiness programme and \nthrough home-based education) is 23.18 lakh as per the Unified District Information \nSystem for Education (UDISE) 2014-15.   \n\n(v) The State Governments and UT Administrations are supported on several interventions \nto improve teaching standards, including regular in-service teachers’ training, induction \ntraining for newly recruited teachers, training of all untrained teachers to acquire \nprofessional qualifications through Open Distance Learning (ODL) mode, recruitment \nof additional teachers for improving pupil-t eacher ratios, academic support for teachers \nthrough block and cluster resource centres,  continuous and comprehensive evaluation \nsystem to equip the teacher to measure pupil performance and provide remedial action \nwherever required, and teacher and school grants for development of appropriate \nteaching-learning materials, etc.  \n****** \n \n \n   \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO.319 TO BE ANSWERED ON 8 TH AUGUST, 2016 ASKED BY SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL \nREGARDING FUNDS FOR RIGHT TO EDUCATION \n \nStatement showing details of funds requested/proposed by the State Governments and UT \nAdministrations for the SSA-RTE programme \n                                    (Rs. in lakh) \nSl. No. State/UT 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n1 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 1402.96 1304.73 2267.91 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 487590.54 420434.43 326530.32 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 59705.51 56504.04 73471.09 \n4 Assam 389626.75 274281.24 347243.70 \n5 Bihar 1252147.01 1333751.47 1827721.95 \n6 Chandigarh 8398.12 7707.19 7865.802 \n7 Chhattisgarh 295850.89 338234.83 330208.12 \n8 Dadar & Nagar Haveli 2721.31 2422.43 4191.27 \n9 Daman & Diu 1154.60 1068.60 1126.50 \n10 Delhi 29812.53 32671.202 36457.876 \n11 Goa 3257.48 3012.58 3229.65 \n12 Gujarat 273720.28 293658.66 336553.43 \n13 Haryana 155421.39 139855.56 158169.8203 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 62245.24 54851.46 66691.18414 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 243476.00 319011.07 309855.61 \n16 Jharkhand 328947.63 307020.34 314433.62 \n17 Karnataka 302974.61 223316.82 317663.273 \n18 Kerala 126816.58 68432.02 72054.95 \n19 Lakshadweep 438.18 436.60 434.65 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 897255.02 804435.838 883365.385 \n21 Maharashtra 215102.06 361791.00 428564.87 \n22 Manipur 63165.89 67111.77 49079.08 \n23 Meghalaya 49968.83 35690.08 48679.29 \n24 Mizoram 29450.30 21358.73 