    "ls|LS16_S9_U_388": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.388\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016\nNORMS FOR MEDICAL COLLEGES\n*388. DR. P. VENUGOPAL:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the salient features of the policy/ guidelines for setting up of medical colleges;\n(b) whether the Government plans to relax the policy/norms for setting up of new\nmedical colleges, along with increasing post graduate medical seats and if so, the\ndetails thereof; and\n(c) the extent to which the new measures are likely to be helpful in tackling the\nproblem of shortage of medical colleges, doctors and especially the specialist, etc.?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 388* FOR 12TH AUGUST, 2016\n(a) The eligibility and qualifying criteria for opening of a new medical college is\nlaid down in Establishment of Medical College Regulations, 1999. There are separate\nminimum requirements for 50/100/150/200/250 students admission and are contained\nin the Minimum Standard Requirements for the Medical Co llege Regulations, 1999.\nThe salient features of these Regulations are as follows:\ni) Prior permission of Central Government/State Government is mandatory\nfor establishment of Medical College.\nii) Other than the Central/State Governments, autonomous bodies promot ed\nby Central and State Governments for the purposes of med ical education\nand University can set up such colleges. A medical college can also be\nset up by any Society/Trust and Company.\niii) An application for opening a new medical college has to be submitted to\nthe Central Government as per requirements laid down in the\nEstablishment of Medical College Regulations, 1999. Government\nforwards the application to the MCI.\niv) The MCI evaluates the application for opening a new medical college by\nconsidering the documents given by the applicant institution and also by\ncarrying out physical inspection.\nv) The permission to establish a medical college and to admit students is\ngranted initially for a period of one year and is renewed on yearly basis\nsubject to fulfillment of requirements.\nvi) The MCI carries out inspections for annual renewal of permission till\nsuch time the establishment of the medical college and expansion of the\nhospital facilities are complete and a formal recognition to the medical\ncollege is granted.\nvii) The process of application and grant of permission is completed in a time\nbound manner as per notified schedule in this regard.\n(b) & (c) Some of the steps taken by the Government for expansion of medical\neducation in the country are as follows:\ni) The ratio of teachers to students has been revised from 1:1 to 1:2 for all\nMD/MS disciplines and 1:1 to 1:3 in subjects of Anaesthesiology,\nForensic Medicine, Radiotherapy, Medical Oncology, Surgical Oncology\nand Psychiatry.\n\nii) DNB qualification has been recognized for appointment as faculty to take\ncare of shortage of faculty.\niii) Enhancement of maximum intake capacity at MBBS level from 150 to\n250.\niv) Enhancement of age limit for appointment/extension/re -employment\nagainst posts of teachers/dean /principal/ dir ector in medical colleges\nfrom 65 to70 years.\nv) Relaxation in the norms for setting up of a medical college in terms of\nrequirement for land, faculty, staff, bed/ bed strength and other\ninfrastructure.\nvi) Strengthening/upgradation of State Government Medical C olleges for\nstarting new PG courses/Increase of PG seats with fund sharing between\nthe Central and State Government.\nvii) Establishment of New Medical Colleges by upgrading district/referral\nhospitals preferably in underserved districts of the country with fund\nsharing between the Central Government and States.\nviii) Strengthening/ upgradation of existing State Government/Central\nGovernment Medical Colleges to increase MBBS seats with fund sharing\nbetween the Central Government and States.\nix) Opening of new AIIMS like institution.\nThe capacity of UG seats has gone up from 52000 to 59800 UG seats and from\n24196 PG seats to, 26390 PG seats in 2016 -17 as compared to 2013-14. During the\nlast three years, as many as 52 new Medical Colleges have been established.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_389": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial Services \nLOK SABHA\nStarred Question No. *389\n                     To be answered onAugust 12, 2016/Sravana21, 1938 (Saka)\nLoans from Private Money Lenders\n*389. SHRI NALIN KUMAR KATEEL: \nSHRI NITYANAND RAI: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n (a) whether the Government is aware that the poor particularly farmers are being \nexploited by the private money lenders and being charged high rate of interest and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(b) whether the Government has received any reports/complaints of their exploitation by \nprivate money lenders and if so, the details thereof for the last three years and the \ncurrent year along with the action taken thereon, State/UTwise; \n(c) whether the Government proposes to bring any legislation to regulate the functioning \nof private money lenders in the country and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the effective steps taken/being taken by the Government in this regard? \nAnswer\nFinance Minister\n(Shri ArunJaitley)\n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (d) of Lok Sabha Starred Question \nNo.*389 to be answered on 12th August, 2016 regarding Loans from Private Money \nLenders\n(a) to (d):The activity of money lending is regulated by State-specific money lending \nlaws. \nThe following measures have, inter alia, been taken to extend institutional credit to \nfarmers/individuals so as to reduce their dependence on non-institutional sources:\ni. The Priority Sector Lending (PSL) directions of RBI mandate all Domestic \nScheduled Commercial Banks to earmark 18% of their Adjusted Net Bank Credit \n(ANBC) or Credit Equivalent amount of Off-Balance Sheet Exposure (OBE), \nwhichever is higher, as on the corresponding date of the previous year, for \nlending to Agriculture including loans to farmers. Further, a sub-target of 8% for \nsmall and marginal farmers has also been fixed.\nii. Loans to distressed farmers and to other persons (not exceeding ₹1,00,000/- per \nborrower)indebted to non-institutional lenders is an eligible category under PSL, \nto enable them to repay their debt to non-institutional lenders.  \niii. The Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY) was launched in April, 2015. Under \nthe scheme, a loan of upto Rs. 50,000/- is given under sub-scheme ‘Shishu’, \nbetween Rs.50,000/- to Rs.5.0 lakhs under sub-scheme ‘Kishore’, and between \nRs.5.0 lakhs to Rs.10.0 lakhs under sub-scheme ‘Tarun’. These measures are \naimed to provide credit to small traders and entrepreneurs to bring them into the \nformal banking system. \niv. To bring small, marginal, tenant farmers, oral lessees, etc. into the fold of \ninstitutional credit, Joint Liability Groups (JLGs) have been promoted by banks.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_390": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF AYURVEDA, YOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA\nAND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH)\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.390\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016\nINTERNATIONAL CAMPAIGN ON AYUSH\n*390. SHRI HARI OM PANDAY:\nSHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA:\nWill the Minister of AYURVEDA, YOGA AND NATUROPATHY,\nUNANI, SIDDHA AND HOMOEOPATHY (AYUSH) be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has any proposals to launch any International\nAdvertisement Campaign to promote AYUSH me dication techniques globally, and\nif so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and\n(b) the extent to which this move is likely to be beneficial in holistic development in\nhealth and pharma sector?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF AYURVEDA,\nYOGA & NATUROPATHY, UNANI, SIDDHA AND\nHOMOEOPATHY(AYUSH)\n(SHRI SHRIPAD YESSO NAIK)\n(a): No such proposal is under consideration of the Government. Such a campaign is\nnot considered advisable in respect of AYUSH medication techniques for the reason\nthat AYUSH systems of medicine involve holi stic /patient specific approach and\nneeds personal supervision of the qualified practitioner.\n(b) Does not arise.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_391": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.391\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016\nCONSUMPTION OF TOBACCO\n*391. SHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:\nDR. SUNIL BALIRAM GAIKWAD:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) the details of quantum of tobacco in the form of cigarettes and bidis including\nsmokeless tobacco consumed in rural and urban areas of the coun try during the last\nthree years;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to convert the unorganised smokeless tobacco\nmanufacturing sectors into organised sectors and if so, the details thereof and if not,\nthe reasons therefor;\n(c) whether the Apex Court h as ordered to ban the sale, purchase and storage of all\nforms of chewable/smokeless tobacco in the country and if so, the details of status of\nimplementation by States/UTs; and\n(d) the steps taken by the Government for rehabilitation of the people who ar e\nworking in the smokeless/chewable tobacco sector?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 391* FOR 12TH AUGUST, 2016\n(a) No such specific data is maintained centrally by the Ministry. However, as per\nthe information received from the Tobacco Board, Department of Commerce, the\nFlue Cured Virgina (FCV) tobacco used for manufacturing of Cigarettes for the last\nthree years is as follows:\n(Quantity in Million Kgs.)\nYear FCV Tobacco\n(Threshed Leaf)\nConsumption/Use\n2013-14 60.97\n2014-15 57.60\n2015-16 (P) 52.29\nNote: (P) - Provisional\n(b) There is no such proposal in the Ministry.\n(c) No such specific order has been given by the Apex Court. However, the\nGovernment of India has issued regulations under the Food Safety & Standards Act\n2006 which lay down that tobacco or nicotine cannot be used as ingredients in food\nproducts.\n(d) As per information received from Department of Agriculture, Cooperation &\nFarmers Welfare, Ministry of Agriculture, Crop Diversification Programme, an\nongoing sub scheme of Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY)  has been extended to\n10 tobacco growing states w.e.f. 2015-16 to encourage tobacco growing farmers to\nshift to alternate crops/cropping systems. An allocation of Rs. 2500 lakh and Rs. 3000\nlakh as central share has been made during 2015 -16 and 2016 -17, respectively to\ndiversify tobacco farming in the s tates of Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Gujarat, Karnataka,\nMaharashtra, Odisha, Tamil Nadu, Telangana, Uttar Pradesh and West Bengal.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_392": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.392\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016\nMEDICAL EQUIPMENT IN PHCS\n*392. KUMARI SUSHMITA DEV:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government is aware that a large number of medical equipment in the\nPublic Health Centres (PHCs) are non-functional thereby causing inconvenience to the\npatients and if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise;\n(b) whether the Government has any monitoring mechanism in place to ensure proper\nmaintenance and operational condition of these equipments and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(c) the funds allocated and utilized for purchase of medical e quipments in PHCs\nduring the last three years, State/UT-wise; and\n(d) the other steps taken by the Government to improve the maintenance of the\nmedical equipments in PHCs?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 392* FOR 12TH AUGUST, 2016\n(a)  to (d) Yes. Government is aware of the dysfunctional equipments in health\nfacilities.  The state wise biomedical equipment data on functionality is placed at\nAnnexure.\nPublic health is a state subject.  However, under National Health Mission,\nGovernment of India (GOI) provides technical and financial support to the States.\nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare, GOI has developed and disseminated the\nGuidelines for Biomedical Equipments Maintenance and M anagement Program. The\nGuidelines provide for outsourcing of Biomedical Equ ipments maintenance with\npayment linked to uptime of equipments.  As per the request for proposal provided\nwith the guidelines, the service provider is required to provide a real time dashboard\nfor monitoring of equipment functionality status.\nData regard ing funds allocated and utilized for purchase of medical equipment in\nPHCs is not maintained centrally by Government of India.\n\nAnnexure\nS.NO Name of States Status of Bio Medical\nEquipment (BME) service\nprovider [Request for\nproposal(RFP)\nreleased/program rolled\nout/RFP in preparation/\nMapping in progress/ No\nprogress]\nApproximate Asset\npvalue\nDysfunctionality\n1 Andhra Pradesh Program rolled out 390 Cr 32%\n2 Arunachal Pradesh Program rolled out 19Cr 35.67%\n3 Assam RFP released 307Cr 28%\n4 Bihar RFP  in preparation 90Cr 30%\n5 Chhattisgarh RFP rolled out 100Cr 34%\n6 Goa RFP in preparation 42Cr 12.46%\n7 Gujarat RFP released 200Cr Not Available\n8 Haryana No progress Not Available Not Available\n9 Himachal Pradesh RFP  in preparation Not available Not Available\n10 Jammu & Kashmir No progress Not available Not available\n11 Jharkhand Program rolled out 144Cr 35%\n12 Karnataka RFP released 430Cr 27%\n13 Kerala Program rolled out 126.87 Cr 15%\n14 Madhya Pradesh RFP released 180 Cr 37%\n15 Maharashtra Program rolled out 440 Cr 30%\n16 Manipur RFP  in preparation 33Cr 35%\n\n17 Meghalaya RFP  in preparation 34 Cr 28%\n18 Mizoram Program rolled out 37Cr 34%\n19 Nagaland RFP issued 26Cr 16.46%\n20 Odisha RFP  in preparation 207Cr 25%\n21 Punjab Program rolled out 67Cr Not available\n22 Rajasthan Program rolled out 330Cr 16%\n23 Sikkim Program rolled out 12 Cr 26.45%\n24 Tamil Nadu No progress Not available Not available\n25 Telangana No progress Not available Not available\n26 Tripura RFP preparation 37Cr 32%\n27 Uttarakhand RFP  in preparation 120Cr 28%\n28 Uttar Pradesh RFP  released 507Cr 28%\n29 West Bengal RFP in preparation 550Cr 25%\n30 Andaman &\nNicobar No progress Not available Not available\n31 Chandigarh RFP issued 6Cr 23.59%\n32 Dadra & Nagar\nHaveli No progress Not available Not available\n33 Daman & Diu NA 8Cr 4.7%\n34 Delhi No progress Not available Not available\n35 Lakshadweep No progress Not available Not available\n36 Puducherry RFP issued Not available Not available",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_393": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.393\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016\nFAMILY WELFARE PROGRAMMES\n*393. SHRI R. PARTHIPAN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has operationalised a decentralised district based\nplanning, monitoring and mid-course correction utilizing the locally generated service\ndata and Civil Registration in the family welfare programmes and if so, the details\nthereof;\n(b) whether the States are actively involved in the above decentralised district based\nplans, if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the extent to which the said approach has contributed to the success of the fa mily\nwelfare programmes?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 393* FOR 12TH AUGUST, 2016\n[a] District-based planning, monitoring and mid course correction is a regular activity\nunder the National Health Mission. The district based planning utilizes the service\ndelivery and survey data. The facility based service data (locally generated) is\ncollected through a web based Health Management Information System (HMIS). All\nmonitoring and review is based on HMIS data uploaded from each district. Based on\nthis information, mid course corrections are suggested to states by the Government of\nIndia.\n[b] Yes. Health is a State subject and states are actively involved in the preparation of\ndistrict action plans which are then consolidated at the state level into State\nProgramme Implementation Plans (PIPs) for onward submission and approval of\nGovernment of India under the National Health Mission (NHM).\n[c] The above approach has helped in streamlining the planning and budgeting process\nwhich has resulted in improved access to quality family welfare services by\naddressing district specific needs.",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_394": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO.394\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016\nMALPRACTICES IN AIIMS\n*394. SHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB:\nSHRI SANJAY DHOTRE:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to\nstate:\n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the instances of corrupt practices/\nmalpractices in the purchase of medical equipments in various Central Gov ernment\nhospitals particularly the All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS) and if so,\nthe details thereof during the last three years, case-wise;\n(b) whether there are reports of improper inquiry conducted into the past cases\nparticularly in AIIMS and if so, the details thereof;\n(c) the reaction of the Government thereto; and\n(d) the action taken by the Government to devise an efficient mechanism to curb such\nmalpractices?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a)  to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 394* FOR 12TH AUGUST, 2016\n(a): As far as three Central Government Hospitals in Delhi viz. Safdarjung Hospital,\nDr. RML Hospital and Lady Hardinge Medical College & Associated Hospitals and\n6 new AIIMS being set up under Phase –I of PMSSY are concerned, no instances of\ncorrupt practices/ malpractices in th e purchase of medical equipment have been\nreported during last three years.\nHowever, as far as AIIMS, New Delhi , is concerned, Central Bureau of\nInvestigation (CBI) had conducted an enquiry into a complaint regarding fraud in\npurchase of surgical items and, on conclusion of enquiry , submitted a Self Contained\nNote in December, 2014, which prima facie pointed to an anomalous financial\npractice for which the officials of AIIMS cited unforeseen emergencies in the field of\nsurgery, necessitating purchases from reputed vendors.\n(b): No.\n(c): Does not arise.\n(d): The inquiry into the complaint did not reveal any corruption, selective\nfavouring or criminal acts. However, it has been decided to streamline the purchase\nprocedure by implementing e-governance, computerisation of inventory and measures\nlike digitisation of store/records with well -trained Store  In -charge who could take\ncare of this as well as other system problems so that the actual items purchased by the\nHospital and available for use could be known. The result of the inquiry and the\nproposed systemic improvement in purchase of equipment/items has been\ncommunicated to Central Vigilance Commission.\n*****",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_396": " \n \n                                     \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n396\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n1\n9\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, \n2016\n \n \nONLINE NATIO\nNAL AGRICULTURAL \nMARKET\n \n \n396. \n \nSHRI\n  \nC.S. PUTTA RAJU:\n \nSHRI\n  \nA.\n \nT. NANA PATIL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes\n \nto set up \nan Online National Agricultural \nMarket an\nd \namend the Agriculture Produce \nMarket Committees (APM\nCs) Act to break \nthe mo\nnopoly of \nmiddlemen/traders\n \n \nand \nprov\nide\n \nbenefits to the farmers and \nconsumers, if so, the details and the\n \no\nbjectives\n \n \nthereof;\n \n \n(b) \n \nwhe\nther the scheme is likely to be \nimplemented in\n \nall the States of the country, \nif so, the \ndetails thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nthe details of the benefits likely to\n \nbe accrue\nd to the farmers and the number \nof \nagric\nultural markets linked with the \ne\n-\nportal at p\nresent in the various States of \nthe country; and\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nthe steps taken/being taken by the\n \nGovern\nment to strengthen the existing \nagricultural \nmarketing infrastructure in th\ne \ncountry?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI  \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nYes, Madam.  Agriculture marketing is administered by State Governments and \nU\nnion Territories (UTs) as per their respective Agriculture Produce Marketing (Regulation) Acts.  \nRealising the need for reforms in the  agri\n-\nmarketing sector, the Government has from time to \ntime played an advocacy role.  To take the  agri\n-\nmarketing reform\n \nagenda forward  and with the \nobjectives to benefit farmers and provide quality produce at reduced cost to consumers,  the \nG\no\nvernment approved a scheme for setting up of National Agriculture Market (e\n-\nNAM)\n \non \n01.07.2015 with a budget of Rs.200 crore.  Unde\nr the scheme, a pan India electronic trading \nportal (e\n-\nNAM) is being deployed in selected regulated wholesale markets in States across the \ncountry and 585 markets are proposed to be integrated  with e\n-\nNAM by March, 2018.\n \n \n \nIn order to integrate\n \nwith\n \ne\n-\nNAM,\n \nStates are required to complete certain  pre\n-\nrequisite \nreforms in their respective Agriculture Produce Marketing (Regulation) Acts to provide for (i) A \nsingle licence to be valid across the State,  (ii)  Single point levy of  market fee, and (iii) \nProvisi\non  for electronic auction as a mode of price discovery.  These reforms, it is expected, will \nfacilitate  integration of markets at the State level to break the monopoly of middlemen/traders \nand provide benefits to the farmers and consumers.\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \nThe  scheme is demand based and applicable to all the States of the country.\n \n \n(c):\n \nAt present, 23 wholesale regulated agricultural markets of 08 States in the country have \nbeen linked with e\n-\nNAM portal.  e\n-\nNAM is expected to benefit the farmers through \nenhanced \nmarket access by way of allowing remote online  bidding for their  produce, removal of \ninformation asymmetry between buyers and sellers, real\n-\ntime price discovery based on the \nactual demand\n-\nsupply gap through transparent online auction, uniform ma\nrketing procedures, \nremuneration according to quality of produce and online  payment directly to farmer’s account.\n \n \n(d):\n \nIn order to implement e\n-\nNAM, the Government is providing software free of cost to the \nthe States  and grant up\n  \nto Rs.30 lakh per mandi for related  infrastructure/equipments \nincluding for assaying.  Apart from this the Government implements various schemes to \nstrengthen agricultural marketing infrastructure viz. Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojna (RKVY), \nAgricultural M\narketing Infrastructure (AMI) sub\n-\nscheme  of Integrated Scheme for Agricultural \nMarketing  (ISAM), Mission for Integrated Develo\np\nment of \nHorticulture\n \n(MIDH),\n \ne\ntc.\n \n \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_397": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE PRODUCTION  \nLOK  SABHA \n  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.397 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 12TH AUGUST, 2016 \n \n \nJOINT  VENTURE  BY  HAL \n \n*397. SHRI  RAM MOHAN NAIDU KINJARAPU:     \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE    j{kk ea=h     \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL) and a French company have \njointly set up a helicopter engines Maintenance, Repair and Overhaul Centre in India ; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof indicating the salient features of the agreement signed for \nthe purpose; \n \n(c)  the ratio of FDI or the equity stakes in the said venture; and \n \n(d)  the extent to which the country is likely to be benefited by such an agreement ? \n                  \n  \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                      (SHRI MANOHAR PARRIKAR) \n     j{kk ea=h                 ¼Jh euksgj ijhZdj½ \n \n(a) to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRE D TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d ) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 397 FOR ANSWER ON 12.8.2016 \n \n(a) Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL) has signed an agreement for \nforming a joint venture with Safran Helicopter Engines (SHE), France \n(formerly known as Turbomeca). \n(b) The salient features of the agreement are as under:- \n• Joint Venture Company shall provide the Support, \nMaintenance, Repair & Overhaul of helicopter engines directly \nto the civil and defence customers. \n \n• HAL and SHE have agreed to incorporate the company with an \nauthorised share capital of Rs. 50 crore.  \n \n(c) The proposed equity ratio is 50:50. \n(d) The Joint Venture Company (JVC) allows synergy of the expertise \nof both HAL and SHE in the field of repair and overhaul (ROH) of \nHelicopter.  It is expected that this partnership will enhance customer \nsatisfaction through improved serviceability, reduced cycle time for \nROH, availability of spares, trouble shooting, field support and \nimprovements / modifications. \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_399": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 399 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nASSISTANCE UNDER NDRF TO KERALA \n \n399.  SHRIMATI P.K. \nSREEMATHI TEACHER: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the amount allocated and released  under the National Disaster \nResponse Fund (NDRF) to Kerala State along with  the utilisation thereof \nduring the last three years; \n \n(b) whether the Kerala Government has  requested for further assistance \nunder NDRF; and \n \n(c) if so, the details and the status thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (c): The primary responsibility for disaster management rests \nwith the States. The concerned State Governments undertak e relief \noperations in the wake of natural disasters from  the State Disaster \nResponse Fund (SDRF) already placed at their disposal in accordance \nwith the GOI approved items & norms of assistance. The Government of \nIndia supplements their efforts by providing assistance from the National \nDisaster Response Fund (NDRF) by following the laid down procedure.  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 399 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \nFinancial assistance under SDRF/ NDRF in the wake of natural disasters \nis by way of relief and not for compensation of loss as suffered/claimed. \n \nThe year-wise details of allocation and releases from SDRF and NDRF to \nthe State Government of Kerala during the last three years is given as \nunder:-   \nSI. No. Year Allocation under \nSDRF \nCenter’s \nshare of SDRF \nreleased \nRelesed from \nNDRF \n1 2013-14 151.74 121.51 61.74 \n2 2014-15 159.33 119.50 0.00 \n3 2015-16 185.00 138.75 0.00 \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_400": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 400 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nBan on Plastic Bags \n \n400 DR. P. VENUGOPAL: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has decided to ban plastic bags thinner than 50 microns; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the rural areas are likely to be brought under the ambit of the said ban; and \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n  \n(a) to (d) Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change has comprehensively revised  \nthe Plastic Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011,  and notified Plastic Waste \n(Management) Rule  in March, 2016. These rules prohibit manufacture, import, stocking, \ndistribution, sale and use of plastic carry bags less than 50 micron in thickness. The rural \nareashave also been brought under the purview of these revised Rules. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_401": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 401 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nFUND ALLOCATION FOR CONSUMER AWARENESS \n \n401. SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI:  \n(OIH) \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the details of the amount allocated for Consumer Awareness Programme  during the \nlast two years; and  \n(b) the amount spent and likely to be spent for the purpose? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n  \n(a) & (b) : The Government carries out “Jago Grahak Jago’’ multi -media campaign for \nconsumer awareness on al India basis through electronic/print media and outdoor publicity. \nThe details of the amount allocated and spend during the last two years  and the current year is \nas under:-  \n \nS.No. Year Amount Allocated  Amount Utilized \n1 2014-15 76.47 (R.E) 71.50 \n2 2015-16 80.00 (R.E) 80.00(Provisional) \n3 2016-17 60.00 (B.E) 44.01 (till date) and  \nremaining amount \nwould be spent  \nwithin the financial \nyear.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_402": " \n \n                                     \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n402\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n1\n9\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, \n2016\n \n \nLICENSING AN\nD FORMATS FOR GM \nTECHNOLOGY AGREEMENT\n \nGUIDELINES\n \n \n402.\n \n \nSHRI OM BIRLA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has\n \nwithdr\nawn the notification calling GM \ndeveloper\ns to \ncompulsorily license their \nproprieta\nry traits on demand, if so, the \ndetails thereof;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nwhether the Government has als\no\n \nrolled back\n \nthe decision not to charge any \nroyal\nty \nexceeding 10 per cent of the \nmaximum \nsale price of seeds, if so, the \ndetails thereof; and\n \n \n(c) \n \nthe details of the key features of the\n \nLicensin\ng and Formats for GM Technology \nAgreement Guidelines and the\n \ntime b\ny \nwhich\n \nthe Government proposes to put \nout a fo\nrmal \nconfirmation on compulsory \nlicense and royalty on sale price of seeds?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI  S\nUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)\n \n \n(a) & (b):\n \nYes, Madam.  The Government has withdrawn on 24\nth\n \nMay, 2016 the Notification \nof Licensing and Formats for GM Technology Agreement Guidelines issued vide S.O. \nNo.1813(E\n) dated 18\nth\n \nMay, 2016.  These licensing guidelines & format for GM Technology \nAgreement have been uploaded on the website of the Department for seeking comments/ \nsuggestions from all stakeholders within 90 days from the date  of uploading on the website \nb\nefore its finalization.\n \n \n(c):\n \nKey features of the Licensing and Formats for GM Technology Agreement Guidelines \ninclude Fair, Reasonable and Non\n-\nDiscriminatory (FRAND) allocation, a cap on maxi\nmum trait \nvalue, no right to ref\nuse grant of license to any eligible seed company fulfilling eligilibity criteria, \nin case of loss of efficacy trait value to be zero, no restrictive condition in the agreement for \nalternative GM technology, etc.\n \n \n \nDecision on these draft Licensing gui\ndeli\nnes will be taken after receiving comments/ \nsuggestions from all stakeholders on completion of 90 days.\n \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_403": " \n \n                                     \nO.I.H\n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n403\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n1\n9\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, \n2016\n \n \nSPURIOU\nS FERTILIZERS\n \n \n403. \n \nSHRI A.T. NANA PATIL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether the Government has made\n \nany assess\nment about the loss suffered by \nthe \nfar\nmers due to large scale sale of \nspurious pe\nsticides and fertilizers in the \ncountry;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nthe concrete steps taken by the \nGovernmen\nt to prevent the production and \nsale of \nspur\nious pesticides and fertilizers \nin various\n \nparts of the country including \nMaharashtra;\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nwhether the Government is\n \nformulatin\ng any scheme for persuading the \nfarme\nrs to a\ndopt \norganic farming instead of \nrel\nying on spurious pesticides and \nfertilizers; and\n \n \n(e)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI  \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)\n \n \n(\na):     No, Madam.\n \n \n(b):\n \n \nQuestion does not arise.\n \n \n(c):\n \n \nUnder the provision of the Insecticides Act, 1968, the Central and State Government \nhave notified 168 and 11,299 number\n \nof Insecticides Inspectors respectively, to carry out \ninspection of manufacturing, storage and sale points etc., draw samples of pesticides and have \nthem analyzed with the help of notified Pesticide Analysts. Prosecution is initiated in competent \ncourts o\nf law in cases of violation of the provisions of the Insecticides Act, 1968. This Act has \nalso vested State Governments with legal authority to investigate complaints and take legal \naction against offenders.\n \n \nThe Fertilizers (Control) Order, 1985 regulate \nthe trade, price, quality and distribution of \nfertilizers. State governments are adequately empowered under this Act to take appropriate \nadministrative and legal actions against those dealers/ manufacturers/ importers who do not \ncomply with the provisions.\n \nFurther, samples of Fertilizer are drawn periodically by the Fertilizer \nInspectors of the State Governments from retailers/\n  \nwholesalers and manufacturing units etc. \nfor testing in the notified laboratories to check their quality.\n \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n(d) & (e)\n:\n \n \nThe Government of India is implementing Paramparagat Krishi Vikas Yojana \n(PKVY) Scheme, under which financial assistance is provided to the farmers for taking up \norganic farming.\n \n \n \nFurther,\n \n \na Central Sector Scheme entitled ‘Mission Organic Val\nue Chain Development \nfor North Eastern Region’ (MOVCDNER) has been launched for development of certified \norganic production in value chain mode in the North Eastern States\n.\n \n \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_404": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n   MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS WELFARE \n             DEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING & FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 404 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016 \nSHORTAGE OF WATER AND FODDER FOR CATTLES \n404. SHRI D.K. SURESH: \n PROF. SAUGATA ROY: \n SHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI: \n DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृ षि एबं कृ िक कल्याण मंत्री  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that 11 States are facing drought and a  large number of farmers \nare facing acute shortage of foodgrains, water for livelihood, irrigation and cattles, if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware that thousands of cows and other cattles  died due to shortage of \nwater and fodder in various States of the country including Rajasthan and Karnataka;  \n \n(c) if so, the corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard; \n \n(d) whether the Government is considering to provide any special package to ove rcome the shortage, \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the details of the assistance provided by the Government to the farmers who lost their livestock, \nState-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a) to (e): Yes Madam. Due to deficit monsoon during the year 2015 -16, 11 States, i.e. Karnataka (both for \nKharif and Rabi), Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra(both for Kharif and Rabi), Odisha, \nTelengana, Uttar Pradesh(both for Kharif and Rabi), Andhr a Pradesh, Jharkhand, Rajasthan and \nUttarakhand (drought Rabi) submitted Memoranda seeking financial assistance from NDRF after \ndeclaring drought in several parts of these States. The details regarding funds approved from the \nNDRF to the drought affected S tates are annexed  as annexure-I. Water levels of 91 major \nreservoirs are monitored  by the Ministry of Water Resources, River Development and Ganga \nRejuvenation.   \nAs per Central Water Commission, the storage position of 91 reservoirs on 6.7.2016 is given below: \ni) Live storage available as on 06.07.2016 in these 91 reservoirs is 28.208 BCM, which is 18% of \ntotal live storage capacity of these reservoirs. This storage is 55% of the storage of \ncorresponding period of last year and 74% of storage of average of last ten years. \nii) Live storage available in 91 reservoirs during the last year was 51.307 BCM. \niii) Average live storage of last ten years in these 91 reservoirs was 38.304 BCM. \n\n \nThe Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and Fisheries is implementing, National Livestock \nMission (NLM) with a budgetary allocation of Rs.292 crore during the year 2016 -17 with a sub-Mission on \nFeed and Fodder Development where financial assistance is provided to all States/UTs, including drought \naffected States. \n  \nDepartment of Agriculture, Co -operation and Farmers Welfare   is implementing Additional Fodder \nDevelopment Programme (AFDP) as a sub -scheme of Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojna (RKVY) to  mitigate \nthe  adverse impacts of drought in drought affected States  from  2014-15 which is continuing during the \ncurrent  year 2016 -17 with a budgetary allocation of Rs.100 crore. The State -wise budgetary allocation is \nannexed as annexure-II. \nAllocation of subsidized foodgrains to the State/UT Governments is made under National Food \nSecurity Act (NFSA)/Ta rgeted Public Distribution System (TPDS). In addition, foodgrains are also \nallocated to States/UTs for natural calamity including drought, festivals, etc. on the basis of requests \nreceived from States/Uts Department  of Food and Public Distribution . On receiving r equest from the \nState Governments of Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, \nUttar Pradesh, Jharkhand, Rajasthan and Chattisgarh for additional allocation of foodgrains for distribution \nto the beneficiaries in the drought affected areas and as per the demand made by them, a quantity of 23.80 \nlakh tons of foodgrains was allocated during 2015-16. Foodgrain allocation for drought relief during 2015 -\n16 and 2016-17 State-wise is annexed as annexure-III. \nIn order to make foodgrains available to the people at cheaper rates, Government of India has enacted \nNational Food Security Act (NFSA) under which foodgrains are allocated to the States/UTs for \ndistribution to eligible beneficiaries @ 5kg per person per month for pri ority households and @ 35kg per \nfamily per month for Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY) households at highly subsidized prices of Re1/ -, \nRs.2/- and Rs.3/- per kg for coarse grains, wheat and rice respectively. The act provides for coverage of \nabout 67% of the population of the country (75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population). With the \nimplementation of NFSA, Government of India is making substantial foodgrain allocation to the \nStates/UTs at highly subsidized prices. During the current year, Government of India has so far allocated a \nquantity of 487.42 lakh tons of foodgrains under NFSA and 50.56 lakh tons of foodgrains under erstwhile \nTPDS to the States/UTs which have not implemented NFSA. In addition, during the current year a \nquantity of 1.87 lakh tons has been allocated for Above Poverty Line (APL) & Below Poverty Line (BPL) \nfamilies (in Non NFSA States) and a quantity of 1.72 lakh tons of foodgrains has also allocated for festivals \nand other additional requirements of the States. Government has also al located a quantity of 37.42 lakh \ntons of foodgrains under Other Welfare Schemes of the Government of India such as Mid Day Meal \n(MDM) scheme, Wheat Based Nutrition Programme (WBNP), Sabla, Annapurna Scheme, etc. \nThe State Government is empowered to underta ke necessary relief measures with the help of State \nDisaster Response Fund (SDRF) which is readily available with them in the wake of notified natural \ncalamities like drought, hailstorm, pest attack cold wave/frost etc. For a calamity of severe nature, \nadditional financial assistance, over and above SDRF, is considered as per extant norms from National \nDisaster Response Fund (NDRF), upon receipt of memorandum from the State Government. \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise Allocation of funds under RKVY of the Additional Fodder Development Programme \n(AFDP) sub -scheme during 2016 -17 with reference to Lok Sabha Question No.404 raised by \nS/Shri D.K.Suresh, Prof. Saugata Roy, Shivkumar Udasi and Dr.Manoj Rajoria due f or answer on \n19.7.2016.  \nState & Union Territories Proposed Allocation of funds (₹ in crore) \nAndhra Pradesh 4.00 \nBihar 3.10 \nChhattisgarh 4.32 \nGoa 0.12 \nGujarat 8.0 \nHaryana 0.60 \nHimachal Pradesh 0.60 \nJammu & Kashmir 0.60 \nJharkhand 1.30 \nKarnataka 9.00 \nKerala 2.00 \nMadhya Pradesh 9.70 \nMaharashtra 18.00 \nOdisha 4.20 \nPunjab 0.64 \nRajasthan 14.10 \nTamil Nadu 2.70 \nTelangana 4.00 \nUttar Pradesh 4.40 \nUttarakhand 0.50 \nWest Bengal 2.80 \nNER States  \nArunachal Pradesh 0.21 \nAssam 3.50 \nManipur 0.40 \nMeghalaya 0.29 \nMizoram 0.11 \nNagaland 0.39 \nSikkim 0.16 \nTripura 0.26 \nTotal 100.00 \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nFoodgrain allocation for drought relief during 2015 -16 and 2016 -17 with reference to Lok Sabha \nQuestion No.404 raised by S/Shri D.K.Suresh, Prof. Saugata  Roy, Shivkumar Udasi and Dr.Majoj \nRajoria due for answer on 19.7.2016. \nStates Quantity Allocated (in tons) \n Rice Wheat Total \nMaharashtra 163000 244000 407000 \nAndhra Pradesh 102312 0 102312 \nChattisgarh 82000 0 82000 \nGujarat 8000 48000 56000 \nJharkhand 171103 21844 192947 \nKarnataka 2577 2556 5133 \nMadhya Pradesh 24000 72000 96000 \nTelangana 250292 0 250292 \nRajasthan 0 1138200 1138200 \nUttar Pradesh 28073 22961 51034 \nTotal 831357 1549561 2380918 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-III \nAssistance Approved from National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF) for Drought and Hailstorm \nwith reference to Lok Sabha  Question No.404 raised by S/Shri D.K.Suresh, Prof. Saugata Roy, \nShivkumar Udasi and Dr.Majoj Rajoria due for answer on 19.7.2016. \n(Rs. in crore) \nDuring – 2015-16  \nS.No. State Calamity Amount approved \n1. Karnataka Drought \nDrought-R \n1540.20 \n723.23 \n2. Chhattisgarh Drought 1276.25 \n3. Madhya Pradesh Drought 2032.68 \n4. Maharashtra Drought-K \nDrought-K -Supplementary \nDrought-R \n3049.36 \n# \n# \n5. Odisha Drought 815.00 \n6. Telangana Drought 791.21 \n7. Uttar Pradesh Drought \nDrought-R \n1304.52 \n# \n8. Andhra Pradesh Drought 433.77 \n9. Jharkhand Drought 336.94 \n10. Rajasthan Drought \nHailstorm \n1193.41 \n# \n11. Uttarakhand Drought-R # \nR:Rabi \n#: Proposals are at various stages of processing. \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_405": " \n \n                                     \nO.I.H\n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n405\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n1\n9\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, \n2016\n \n \nMOBILE \nVENDING CARTS SERVICE\n \n \n405. \n \nSHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH\n \nJOSHI:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether the Government has started\n \nmobile vendin\ng carts service for the farmers \nin \nvarious States of the country;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nif \nso, the details and the salient \nfeatures of \nthe service along with the time \nb\ny which it is \nlikely to be implemented; \n \n \n(c)\n \n \nthe details regard\ning the percentage \nof farmers l\nikely to be benefited under the \nscheme; \nand\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nwhether there is any proposal\n \npending with\n \nthe Government in this regard, \nif so, the \ndetails thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI  \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)\n \n \n(a) to (d):\n \nThe Government is implementing the Mission for Integrated Development of \nHorticulture (MIDH) for horticulture development which includes promotion of Static/Mobile \nVe\nnding Ca\nrt/platform with cool chamber for\n \nperishable horticulture crops.  The scheme is \ndemand driven and can be availed by farmers as well as private sector entrepreneurs.\n \n \n \nThese three components are demand & entrepreneur driven for which credit linked b\nack \nended subsidy is available through respective State Horticulture Missions.  An entrepreneur \ncan \navail assistance for individual component or combination of different components for which \nassistance is at the rate of 50% with admissible  cost of Rs.30,0\n00/unit.  So far, under the \nscheme 646 units with total assistance of Rs.87.18 lakh have been sanctioned.\n \n \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_406": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 406 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nIndustrial Activities in Western Ghats \n \n406.  SHRIMATI VANAROJA R: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT , FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE  be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Western Ghats are threatened by harmful industrial activities such as mining  \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether it is true  that the Western Ghats support the single largest population of \nendangered Asian elephants and vulnerable Indian bison and if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the harmful industrial development  activities have pose d a threat to the \necosystem services and the communities that depend on them; and \n(d) if so, the corrective steps proposed to be taken by the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n \n(a) The report of the High- Level Working Group (HLWG) headed by Dr. K. Kasturirangan, \nMember of erstwhile Planning Commission has identified the Western Ghats in India as \nthreatened by harmful industrial and other activities including mining.  The industries/a ctivities \ninclude ‘Red’ category of industries, Thermal Power Plants, commercial mining, large -scale \nbuilding constructions and townships.  \n \n(b) Yes, Sir. \n \n(c) and (d) Yes, Sir. Based on the recommendations of the High Level Working Group \n(HLWG) report on West ern Ghats, the Ministry of Environment, Forests and Climate Change \nissued Directions under Section 5 of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 on 13\nth November \n2013, in order to safeguard the Western Ghats and to maintain its environmental integrity. As per \nthe Directions, five categories of new and/or expansion projects/activities which have maximum \ninterventionist and damaging impacts on ecosystems shall not be considered for granting \nenvironmental clearance in the Ecologically Sensitive Area (ESA) of Western Ghats as identified \nby the High Level Working Group. \n \nThe Ministry of Environment, Forests and Climate Change has also published a draft \nNotification dated 4\nth September, 2015 for declaring ecologically sensitive areas (ESAs) in the \nWestern Ghats for prohibiting and regulating activities which have the maximum destructive \npotential and impacts on the Western Ghats \n**** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_407": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 407 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nFUNCTIONING OF CFSL \n \n407.  SHRI RAHUL SHEWALE: \nSHRI SANJAY DHOTRE: \nSHRI BHARTRUHARI MAHTAB: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the number of Central Forensic  Science Laboratories (CFSLs) \nfunctioning in the country, at present, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b) whether the Bureau of Police Research and Development (BPR&D) \nhas conducted training programmes for police personnel with regard to \ncollecting forensic evidence in the country, if so, the total number of \nsuch training programmes conducted during each of the last three years \nand the current year, State/Union Territory wise; \n \n(c) whether there is shortage of manpower in the CFSLs in the country, if \nso, the details thereof, CFSL -wise, cadre-wise and the reasons therefor \nalong with the remedial measures taken/being taken by  the Government \nin this regard; \n \n(d) whether cases of breach of secre cy by the contractual employees \nworking in the said CFSLs have come to the notice of the Government \nduring the said period, if so, the details thereof, CFSL -wise and the \nreasons therefor along with the corrective action taken/being taken by \nthe Government in this regard, so far; and \n \n(e) the other steps taken/being taken by the Government to provide \nadequate skills to the police personnel in collecting forensic evidence, \nadequate manpower in CFSLs and to improve their functioning in the \ncountry? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n...2/- \n \n\n-2- \n \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 407 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \n(a):  There are 6 Central Forensic Science Laboratories (CFSLs) in the \ncountry located at Chandigarh, Hyderabad, Kolkata, Bhopal, Guwahati \nand Pune under Directorate of Forensic Science Services(DFSS).  The 7 th \nCFSL(CBI) is under the control of Central Bureau of Investigation located \nat New Delhi. \n \n(b): Yes, Madam. Bureau of Police Research and Development (BPR&D) \nhas conducted training programmes for police personnel with regard to \ncollecting forensic evidence in the country at Rajasthan, Uttar Pradesh, \nWest Bengal & Andhra Pradesh and at Union Territory of Chandigarh.  \nThe year-wise details are as under : \n \nIn 2013-14 : Conducted 05 Courses \nIn 2014-15 : Conducted 03 Courses \nIn 2015-16 : Conducted 01 Course \nIn 2016-17 : 06 Courses (Proposed) \n \nUnder the  project ‘Developing Specialist Investigators’ following \ncourses were also organized by BPR&D to provide training to police \npersonnel in Forensic Science:  \n..3/- \n\n-3- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 407 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \nIn 2013-14 : 07 Courses \nIn 2015-16 : 03 Courses  \nIn 2016-17 : 03 Courses (Yet to be   conducted) \n \n(c): There is adequate manpower available in all CFSLs.  However \nduring the12 th Five Year Plan, three new CFSLs at Pune, Bhopal and \nGuwahati have been established with provision for man power alongwith \nupgradation of  examination facilities in existing three CFSLs at \nChandigarh, Hyderabad and Kolkata. Forty three number of \nscientific/technical manpower has been recruited on contract basis and \nposted in all the CFSLs for smooth functioning. \n \n(d):  No case of breach of secrecy by the contractual employees \nworking in the CFSLs has come to the notice of the Government. \n \n(e): Besides BPR&D, Lok Nayak Jayaprakash Narayan National \nInstitute of Criminology and Forensic Science (LNJN NICFS), New Delhi \nunder the Ministry of Home Affairs is constantly organizing training for \nPolice to educate them for collection and examination of forensic \nevidence. NICFS has designed several courses to provide skills and \nknowledge to police  \n\n…4/- \n-4- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 407 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \npersonnel in collecting forensic evidence.  During the last 5 years, NICFS \nhas trained 1378 police personnel. NICFS has also published  Standard \nOperating Procedure to fill the gap between police personnel and \nforensic scientists and guide police personnel in collection, \npreservation, packaging and forwarding of forensic evidence to forensic \nscience laboratories. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_408": " \n \n \n                                 \n                                      \n \n                                                                             \n    \nO.I.H\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, C\nOOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n408\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n1\n9\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, \n2016\n \n \nSETTING UP OF SEED CENTRES\n \n \n408.\n \n \n \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR\n \nSINGH:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nthe number of existing seed centres/\n \nkendras in the country and the programmes \n \nbeing \nc\nonducted by these seed centres, \nState\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nwhether any seed centre has been\n \nset u\np in Chatra, Latehar and \nPalamu regions of \nJharkhand, if so, the details \nthereof; and\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nif not, the time by which these seed\n \ncentres are likely to be set up?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI  \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)\n \n \n(a):\n \nThe State wise details of existing Seed Centres/Kendras and the programmes b\neing \nconducted are at Annexure\n.\n \n \n(b) & (c):\n \nYes, Madam.   There are four Seed Producing agencies namely, Gram Lok Kalyan \nKendra, Bhartiya\n \nJan Kalyan Kendra, Priyanka Phal Phool Sabji Beej Utpadak Samiti, Bhadra \nKali Swayam Sahayata Samooh in Chatra district, two in Latehar district namely, Ujjwala \nKissan Club and Sarvoday Beej Gram, Latehar and two in Palamu district, namely, Krishi Vikas \nK\nendra and Beer Kunwar Singh Kissan Swayam Sahayata Samooh,\n \nPalam\nu that are involved \nin Seed Production Programme.\n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n \nThe State\n-\nwise details of Seed Centres/Kendras/Sale outlets\n \nState\n \nDetails\n \nJharkhand\n \n69 seed producing agencies were involved i\nn seed production \nprogramme in the state.  There are 1985 seed dealers in the state.\n \nHaryana\n \n75 sales outlets of Haryana Seed Development Corporation and 7671 \nprivate seed dealers exist in the state.\n \nBihar\n \nTwo State Agriculture Universities,39 Krishi Vig\nyan Kendras,12 Bihar \nRajya Beej Nigam Limited, National Seeds Corporation, KRIBHCO, \nHindustan Insecticide Limited, National Horticulture Research \nDevelopment Foundation are doing seed production and marketing in \npublic sector.\n \n14\n-\n \nPrivate Seed Company prod\nuced seed in Bihar and marketing.\n \n132 State level seed licenses have been issued.\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \nTotal 8361 seed centres/kendras of Government/Semi Government and \n19235 seed dealer centres  in private sector  exists  in Uttar Pradesh for \nsupply of seeds.\n \nTamil Nadu\n \nExisting Seed Centres/outlets include Government\n-\n1369, Quasi  \ngovernment \n-\n1,392, private\n-\n6501. Total \n-\n \n9,262  outlets exists for supply \nof quality inputs viz. seeds, bio\n-\nfertilizers, bio\n-\npesticides, micro nutrient \nmixtures and farm implements.\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n1333 primary co\n-\noperative societies exists for supplying seeds and \nfertilizers to the farmers and 1601 private seed centres exist in the State.\n \nMadhya \nPradesh\n \n10747 number of existing seed centres/kendras in Madhya Pradesh .\n \nSeed production a\nnd distribution programme are being conducted by \nsome of these centres and sale programmes are being conducted by \nsome of these centres.\n \nKerala\n \nKerala State Seed Development Authority works for the procurement \nand supply of quality seeds to the farmers.  \n \nSeed production and supply \nare also being carried out at various seed farms under the Department \nof Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, Kerala and Kerala Agricultural \nUniversity Research Station under ICAR seed project and state plan \nschemes.\n \nOdisha\n \n3,350 Pri\nmary Agriculture Co\n-\noperative Societies, 7 existing seed \ncentres of Odisha State Seed Corporation and 3653 private seed \ndealers are there for supply of seeds.\n \nAndhra Pradesh\n \n13 Andhra Pradesh State Seed Development Corporation sale counters, \n819 co\n-\noperat\nives societies outlets and 5,788 private seed dealers exist \nfor supply of seeds.\n \nGujarat\n \n15\n-\n \nGujarat State Seeds Corporation Ltd.  sale counters, 535\n-\n \nCo\n-\noperatives sale counters and 14765\n-\nprivate seed dealers outlets \n-\n \nTotal\n-\n15315 outlets exist for seed \nsupply.\n \nKarnataka\n \n \nKarnataka State Seed Corporation\n-\n34 seed sale counters, Karnataka \nOilseeds Grower’s Co\n-\noperatives \n-\n62 seed sale counters and 5,856 \nprivate seed sale counters exist for seed supply.\n \nPunjab\n \nPunjab State Seed Corporation has 3 seed sale c\nentres, Punjab \nAgricultural University \n–\n \nKrishi Vigyan Kendras \n-\n \n22 seed sale centres \nand 146 Blocks have seed sale counters  for public/farmers. \n \nTelangana\n \n1016\n-\n \nSubsidy seed sale counters, 9 \n-\n \n(University Agriculture Research \nStation(ARS) sale counters,\n \n20\n-\nKrishi Vigyan Kendras (KVK) and 6,032 \nprivate dealers sale counters. Total\n-\n7,077.\n \nAssam\n \n27  Assam Seeds Corporation Ltd. Sales outlets, 23 \n-\n  \nKrishi Vigyan \nKendras (KVK) sale outlets under  State Agriculture Universities exist. \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \nU\nttrakhand\n \n695 Department of Agriculture seeds outlets and 15 seed centres  \ncomprising of  National Seeds Corporation Ltd., Uttaranchal State   & \nTarai Development Corporation Ltd , NAFED and IFFCO are there for \nproviding seeds to the farmers as well as  81\n6 private seed outlets. \n \nRajasthan\n \n22\n-\n \nSeed processing plants cum sales outlets of Rajasthan State Seeds \nCorporation Ltd.(RSSC), 2\n-\nSales depot ,9\n-\n \nExtension  centres of RSSC, \n120 Kraya Vikrya  Samities  and 5000 Gram Seva Sahkari Samities \nprovide seeds to\n \nthe farmers.\n \nMaharashtra\n \n26\n-\n \nMaharashtra State Seed Corporation sales offices, 310\n-\n \nTaluka \nCooperative Societies sales outlets, 532\n-\n \nState seed dealers, 34,874 \n–\nDistrict level  seed dealers, Total\n-\n35,406 and 134 \n-\n \nCotton seed dealers \nexist for sale of se\neds. \n \nHimachal \nPradesh\n \nExisting sales counters/outlets in Government sector\n-\n266, State \nAgriculture University \n-\n1 and   private seed sales outlets \n-\n670 for sale of \nseeds. \n \nDelhi\n \n \nOne sale counter each at National Seeds Corporation Ltd and Indian \nAgricultu\nre Research Institute (IARI) and  82  Private seed outlets for \nsale of seeds. \n \nPuducherry\n \n60   seed dealers exist for sale of seeds. \n \nTripura\n \n390   seed centres /outlets under   Department of Agriculture and 35 \nseed outlets exist in private sector for sa\nle of seeds. \n \nWest Bengal\n \n14 \n-\n \nWest Bengal   State Seed Corporation , 129\n-\n \nSeed Producing \nOrganisation , 3 State Agriculture Universities,18\n-\n \nKrishi Vigyan Kendras \n(KVK)  and 9,000 seed dealers exist for sale of seeds. \n \nMeghalaya\n \n11 \n–\nDistrict Agriculture Office outlets, 39 Block Development Office \noutlets and 8 private seed dealer outlet  exists for sale of seeds\n \nNational Seeds \nCorporation Ltd.\n \nNSC has a total 66 area office cum seed sale counters.  State\n-\n  \nwise \nArea Office cum sa\nle counters are as follows :\n \nTwo each in Punjab, Haryana, Odisha, Telangana and Kerala, three \neach in Madhya Pradesh, West Bengal, Andhra Pradesh and \nTamilnadu, one each in Delhi, Uttrakhand, Gujarat, Chhattisgarh, \nJharkhand, Sikkim and Assam, and eight ea\nch in Uttar Pradesh, \nRajasthan and Karnataka, Seven in Maharashtra and Six in Bihar State.\n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_409": " \n \n                                     \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n409\n \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n1\n9\nTH\n \n \nJULY\n, \n2016\n \n \nAGREEMENTS B\nETWEEN INDIA AND \nTAIWAN IN AGRICULTURE\n \n \n409. \n \n    \nSHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO\n \nRAUT:\n \n    \nSHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether the Government has signed\n \na Me\nmorandum of Understanding (MoU) \nwith \nTaiwan in the field of agriculture;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nif so\n, the details and the salient \nfeatures thereof;\n \n \n(c)\n \n \nwhether both the countri\nes have\n \nidentified the\n \npriority areas for cooperation \nin the field of \nagriculture;\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof; and\n \n \n(e)\n \n \nthe ext\nent to which it is likely to be \nbeneficial\n \nto the country and the time by \nwhich it will \nbe implemented?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER\n \nOF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ालय म\u0000 रा\u0000य मं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI  \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)\n \n \n(a):\n \nNo, Madam.\n \n \n(b) to (e):\n \nHowever, the Government have approved the text of the proposed MoU for \nsignature as per mutual convenience of  both countries.\n \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_410": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 410 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nCOMPENSATION FOR VICTIMS OF EMERGENCY \n \n410.  SHRI GOPAL SHETTY: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the families of citizens  tortured and imprisoned in many \nStates in  the country during the Emergency declared  in the year 1977 \nare being given a monthly allowance as compensation; \n \n(b) if so, the amount of financial  assistance being provided to such \nfamilies along with the date from which the said  assistance is being \nprovided, State-wise; \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes  to prepare a State -wise list of \nsuch families at the Central level and provide uniform amount of monthly \nCentral assistance across all States to all such families; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) to (d):  At present no monthly allowance as compensation is being \ngiven to the families of citizens tortured and imprisoned during the \nEmergency in the year 1977. Further there is no such proposal under \nconsideration of the Government to give monthly allowance or grant \nfinancial assistance to such families. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_411": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n411\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n               \nPRODUCTION OF WHEAT\n \n      \n \n411. \n \nSHRIMATI RITI PATHAK: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether a substantial decline has been registered in the production of wheat in \nthe current year in comparison to that of last two years and if so, the details thereof, \nState\n-\nwise; and \n \n \n(b) \n \nthe steps being taken by the Government to encourage the farmers\n \nto increase \nthe production of wheat with the help of various agricultural schemes, Minimum Support \nPrice and crop insurance schemes?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT \n)  \n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \n \nAfter achieving record production of 95.85 million tonnes of wheat in 2013\n-\n14, \ntotal production in wheat in the country during 2014\n-\n15 declined to 86.53 million tonnes\n. \nThe decline in production of wheat during 2014\n-\n15 has been mainly on account of un\n-\nseasonal rains/hailstorms during February/March 2015. Despite unfavorable \ntemperature conditions at the time of sowing as well as at the time of flowering of wheat \nand def\nicient monsoon rainfall leading to problem of water shortage in reservoirs, total \nproduction of wheat in the country is estimated at 94.04 million tonnes (3\nrd\n \nAdvance \nEstimates) i.e. an increase of 7.51 million tonnes over the previous year.\n \n \n \nThe State\n-\nwise details of the production of wheat in the country during 2013\n-\n14 \nto 2015\n-\n16 are given in \nAnnexure. \n \n \n(b):\n \nIn order to increase production of wheat in the country, the Government of India \nis implementing through State Governments, several Cro\np Development \nSchemes/Programmes such as \nN\national Food Security Mission (NFSM) \n–\n \nWheat, \nRashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA) etc.\n \n \n \nTo achieve higher productivity of agricultural crops including wheat, Indian \nCouncil of \nAgricultural Research (ICAR) is conducting basic and strategic research \nrelating to crop improvement, production and protection technologies suitable to \ndifferent situations including development of location\n-\nspecific varieties/hybrids and \ntechnologies.\n \n \n \nF\nurther, to encourage farmers for cultivation of wheat by ensuring remunerative \nprices, the Government has increases Minimum Support Price (MSP) of wheat from \nRs.1450/\n-\n \nper quintal during Rabi \nM\narketing Season (RMS), 2015\n-\n16 to Rs.1525/\n-\n \nper \n quintal for RMS , 2016 - 17.  \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nProduction of Wheat in India during 2013\n-\n14 to 2015\n-\n16\n \n \n                                                             \nProduction (‘000 Tonnes)\n \n                                           \n \nState/ UT\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16*\n \n1\n \n2\n \n3\n \n4\n \nAssam\n \n40.4\n \n28.8\n \n38.0\n \nBihar  \n \n4738.0\n \n3987.0\n \n5251.1\n \nChhatisgarh\n \n134.0\n \n135.3\n \n136.4\n \nGujarat\n \n4694.0\n \n3059.0\n \n2630.0\n \nHaryana\n \n11800.0\n \n10354.0\n \n11489.0\n \nHimachal Pradesh  \n \n670.7\n \n646.5\n \n494.1\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n601.9\n \n314.3\n \n497.4\n \nJharkhand  \n \n370.4\n \n330.4\n \n274.4\n \nKarnataka\n \n210.0\n \n261.0\n \n163.0\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n12937.0\n \n17103.9\n \n15644.1\n \nMaharashtra\n \n1602.0\n \n1308.0\n \n718.0\n \nPunjab\n \n17620.0\n \n15050.0\n \n16512.0\n \n \nRajasthan\n \n8663.2\n \n9823.9\n \n10002.7\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n29890.9\n \n22417.4\n \n28365.0\n \nUttarakhand\n \n842.4\n \n654.2\n \n755.0\n \nWest Bengal\n \n927.8\n \n939.3\n \n945.0\n \nOthers\n \n107.0\n \n113.8\n \n120.6\n \nAll India                             \n95849.8\n \n86526.6\n \n94035.8\n \n* As per Third Advance Estimates. \n \n \n \n     \n\n*********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_413": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 413 \nANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nFOOD PROCESSING UNITS \n \n413.  SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA:  \nSHRI DUSHYANT CHAUTALA:  \nSHRI RAJU SHETTY:  \nSHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA:  \nSHRI VISHNU DAYAL RAM:  \nSHRI RAHUL KASWAN:  \nSHRI ALOK SANJAR: \n \n Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of Food Processing Units in the country, State-wise; \n(b)  the details of Centrally Sponsored Schemes being implemented for setting up of Food \nProcessing Units in the country; \n(c)   whether there has been a shortage of Food Processing Units in the country resulting in \nfarmers not getting the right price for their produce, if so, the details thereof and the  \nreaction of the Government in this regard;  \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to prepare a blueprint to identify suitable locations for \nthe setting up of Food Processing Units in the country, if so, the details thereof; \n(e)   the details of the Food Processing Units proposed to be set up in the country, State -wise; \nand \n(f)    whether the Government proposes to formulate any scheme for development and \npromotion of various fruit and vegetable growing areas in the country to provide better \nraw material to Food Processing Units, if so, the details thereof, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a): As per Annual Survey of Industries (ASI), 2013 -2014, the total number of food \nprocessing units regis tered in the country is 37445.  The state -wise number of food processing \nunits in the country is given in Annexure.    \n  \n\n(b) For the purpose of setting up of food processing units in the country, Ministry of Food \nProcessing Industries had been implementing  a Scheme namely Scheme for Technology \nUpgradation/ Establishment/ Modernization of Food Processing Industries up to 31.03.2012.  \nUnder this scheme, financial assistance in the form of grant -in-aid was being provided for \nSetting up/Technology Upgradation/Modernization of food processing units in the country. The \nquantum of financial assistance admissible for eligible entrepreneurs under the above scheme \nwas  at the rate of 25% of the cost of Plant & Machinery and Technical Civil Works subject to a \nmaximum of Rs. 50 lakhs in case of General areas and for difficult areas, the same was at rate of \n33.33% of the cost of Plant & Machinery and Technical Civil Works subject to a maximum of \nRs. 75 lakhs.  The aforesaid scheme was subsequently subsumed in the Centrall y Sponsored \n(CSS)-National Mission on Food Processing (NMFP) with effect from 01.04.2012 till \n31.03.2015.  Thereafter, the said scheme got delinked from Government of India’s assistance \nand it was left to the State Governments to decide on its continuance from their increased \nresource as per recommendation of 14th Finance Commission.  \n \n  As a part of the Committed / Spillover liabilities of 11 th Plan period during the 12th Plan \nperiod also, Ministry of Food Processing Industries has been releasing grant -in-aid to eligible \nentrepreneurs under the Scheme for Technology Upgradation/ Establishment/ Modernization of \nFood Processing Industries.   \n \n (c) As per an assessment of the extent of food processing in various food sub -sectors done \nin 2014 by the Institute of Economic Growth on behalf of Ministry of Agriculture, the average \nextent of processing of agro -products in 2010 -11 was 6.76%.  It implies that huge scope \navailable for setting up food processing industries by the interested and eligible entrepreneurs of \nfood processing sector in the country.  The Schemes implemented by the Ministry Food \nProcessing Industries have also contributed to the setting up of food processing units in the \ncountry.   \n  \n(d) & (e)  The Ministry has prepared a food map   showing availa bility of fruits and vegetables \nin different States in the country. The same has been made available in the website of the \nMinistry of Food Processing Industries ( www.mofpi.nic.in) so that the entrepreneurs wishing to \nsetup Food Processing Industries may h ave an idea about the availability of  raw materials in a \nparticular location and thus help them in taking an informed decision in setting up of their Food \nProcessing Industries. \n(f) No. Already the Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH)  is being \nimplemented in the States and Union Territories.  Under this, the fruits and vegetables are being \npromoted as an area expansion and under protected cultivation also as a cluster approach to \nfulfil the requirement of raw material for processing un its and also to fulfil the market demand.  \nThe cultivation of fruits and vegetables are promoted on the basis of agro -climatic conditions \nand market demand also. \n \nApart from that, the government is also promoting fruits and vegetables cultivation in the States \nthrough Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana (RKVY) on the basis of projects submitted to S tates and \nDepartment of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare, Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers \nWelfare. \n \n****** \n \n \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PAR T (a) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 413 FOR ANSWER ON 19TH JULY, 2016  REGARDING FOOD \nPROCESSING UNITS  \n \nState-wise estimated Number of Factories in Food Processing Sector for  2013-14 \nS. \nNo. \nName of the State/UTs Number of registered units \n1 Andhra Pradesh 5,739 \n2 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 5 \n3 Assam 1,294 \n4 Bihar 794 \n5 Chandigarh (U.T.) 19 \n6 Chhattisgarh 1,049 \n7 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 3 \n8 Daman & Diu 31 \n9 Delhi 166 \n10 Goa 86 \n11 Gujarat 1,904 \n12 Haryana 631 \n13 Himachal Pradesh 172 \n14 Jammu & Kashmir 144 \n15 Jharkhand 198 \n16 Karnataka 2,033 \n17 Kerala 1,460 \n18 Madhya Pradesh 672 \n19 Maharashtra 3,040 \n20 Manipur 21 \n21 Meghalaya 18 \n22 Nagaland 15 \n23 0disha 932 \n24 Pudducherry 69 \n25 Punjab 2,786 \n26 Rajasthan 862 \n27 Sikkim 21 \n28 Tamil Nadu 5,204 \n29 Telangana 3,850 \n30 Tripura 71 \n31 Uttar Pradesh 2,037 \n32 Uttarakhand 380 \n33 West Bengal 1,739 \n Total 37,445 \nSource: Annual Survey of Industries, 2013-14 \n \n  ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_414": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 414 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nBIFURCATION OF STATES \n \n414.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has  recently conducted any study to \nexamine the merits/demerits of smaller States including aspects such as \nadministrative efficiency, resource utilisation and financial devolution; \n \n(b) if so, the details and outcome thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any  proposal to constitute a State  \nReorganisation Commission to review the  merits/demerits of creating \nsmaller States,  examine proposals for bifurcation of States  to improve \ngovernance and elaborate criteria  to govern the fut ure \ncreation/bifurcation of States; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a):  No Madam \n(b):  In view of (a) above, question does not arise. \n(c):  No Madam \n(d):  In view of (c) above, question does not arise.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_415": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n   MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS WELFARE \n             DEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING & FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 404 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016 \nSHORTAGE OF WATER AND FODDER FOR CATTLES \n404. SHRI D.K. SURESH: \n PROF. SAUGATA ROY: \n SHRI SHIVKUMAR UDASI: \n DR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE कृ षि एबं कृ िक कल्याण मंत्री  \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that 11 States are facing drought and a  large number of farmers \nare facing acute shortage of foodgrains, water for livelihood, irrigation and cattles, if so, the details \nthereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware that thousands of cows and other cattles  died due to shortage of \nwater and fodder in various States of the country including Rajasthan and Karnataka;  \n \n(c) if so, the corrective steps taken by the Government in this regard; \n \n(d) whether the Government is considering to provide any special package to ove rcome the shortage, \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) the details of the assistance provided by the Government to the farmers who lost their livestock, \nState-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a) to (e): Yes Madam. Due to deficit monsoon during the year 2015 -16, 11 States, i.e. Karnataka (both for \nKharif and Rabi), Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra(both for Kharif and Rabi), Odisha, \nTelengana, Uttar Pradesh(both for Kharif and Rabi), Andhr a Pradesh, Jharkhand, Rajasthan and \nUttarakhand (drought Rabi) submitted Memoranda seeking financial assistance from NDRF after \ndeclaring drought in several parts of these States. The details regarding funds approved from the \nNDRF to the drought affected S tates are annexed  as annexure-I. Water levels of 91 major \nreservoirs are monitored  by the Ministry of Water Resources, River Development and Ganga \nRejuvenation.   \nAs per Central Water Commission, the storage position of 91 reservoirs on 6.7.2016 is given below: \ni) Live storage available as on 06.07.2016 in these 91 reservoirs is 28.208 BCM, which is 18% of \ntotal live storage capacity of these reservoirs. This storage is 55% of the storage of \ncorresponding period of last year and 74% of storage of average of last ten years. \nii) Live storage available in 91 reservoirs during the last year was 51.307 BCM. \niii) Average live storage of last ten years in these 91 reservoirs was 38.304 BCM. \n\n \nThe Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and Fisheries is implementing, National Livestock \nMission (NLM) with a budgetary allocation of Rs.292 crore during the year 2016 -17 with a sub-Mission on \nFeed and Fodder Development where financial assistance is provided to all States/UTs, including drought \naffected States. \n  \nDepartment of Agriculture, Co -operation and Farmers Welfare   is implementing Additional Fodder \nDevelopment Programme (AFDP) as a sub -scheme of Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojna (RKVY) to  mitigate \nthe  adverse impacts of drought in drought affected States  from  2014-15 which is continuing during the \ncurrent  year 2016 -17 with a budgetary allocation of Rs.100 crore. The State -wise budgetary allocation is \nannexed as annexure-II. \nAllocation of subsidized foodgrains to the State/UT Governments is made under National Food \nSecurity Act (NFSA)/Ta rgeted Public Distribution System (TPDS). In addition, foodgrains are also \nallocated to States/UTs for natural calamity including drought, festivals, etc. on the basis of requests \nreceived from States/Uts Department  of Food and Public Distribution . On receiving r equest from the \nState Governments of Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, \nUttar Pradesh, Jharkhand, Rajasthan and Chattisgarh for additional allocation of foodgrains for distribution \nto the beneficiaries in the drought affected areas and as per the demand made by them, a quantity of 23.80 \nlakh tons of foodgrains was allocated during 2015-16. Foodgrain allocation for drought relief during 2015 -\n16 and 2016-17 State-wise is annexed as annexure-III. \nIn order to make foodgrains available to the people at cheaper rates, Government of India has enacted \nNational Food Security Act (NFSA) under which foodgrains are allocated to the States/UTs for \ndistribution to eligible beneficiaries @ 5kg per person per month for pri ority households and @ 35kg per \nfamily per month for Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY) households at highly subsidized prices of Re1/ -, \nRs.2/- and Rs.3/- per kg for coarse grains, wheat and rice respectively. The act provides for coverage of \nabout 67% of the population of the country (75% of the rural and 50% of the urban population). With the \nimplementation of NFSA, Government of India is making substantial foodgrain allocation to the \nStates/UTs at highly subsidized prices. During the current year, Government of India has so far allocated a \nquantity of 487.42 lakh tons of foodgrains under NFSA and 50.56 lakh tons of foodgrains under erstwhile \nTPDS to the States/UTs which have not implemented NFSA. In addition, during the current year a \nquantity of 1.87 lakh tons has been allocated for Above Poverty Line (APL) & Below Poverty Line (BPL) \nfamilies (in Non NFSA States) and a quantity of 1.72 lakh tons of foodgrains has also allocated for festivals \nand other additional requirements of the States. Government has also al located a quantity of 37.42 lakh \ntons of foodgrains under Other Welfare Schemes of the Government of India such as Mid Day Meal \n(MDM) scheme, Wheat Based Nutrition Programme (WBNP), Sabla, Annapurna Scheme, etc. \nThe State Government is empowered to underta ke necessary relief measures with the help of State \nDisaster Response Fund (SDRF) which is readily available with them in the wake of notified natural \ncalamities like drought, hailstorm, pest attack cold wave/frost etc. For a calamity of severe nature, \nadditional financial assistance, over and above SDRF, is considered as per extant norms from National \nDisaster Response Fund (NDRF), upon receipt of memorandum from the State Government. \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise Allocation of funds under RKVY of the Additional Fodder Development Programme \n(AFDP) sub -scheme during 2016 -17 with reference to Lok Sabha Question No.404 raised by \nS/Shri D.K.Suresh, Prof. Saugata Roy, Shivkumar Udasi and Dr.Manoj Rajoria due f or answer on \n19.7.2016.  \nState & Union Territories Proposed Allocation of funds (₹ in crore) \nAndhra Pradesh 4.00 \nBihar 3.10 \nChhattisgarh 4.32 \nGoa 0.12 \nGujarat 8.0 \nHaryana 0.60 \nHimachal Pradesh 0.60 \nJammu & Kashmir 0.60 \nJharkhand 1.30 \nKarnataka 9.00 \nKerala 2.00 \nMadhya Pradesh 9.70 \nMaharashtra 18.00 \nOdisha 4.20 \nPunjab 0.64 \nRajasthan 14.10 \nTamil Nadu 2.70 \nTelangana 4.00 \nUttar Pradesh 4.40 \nUttarakhand 0.50 \nWest Bengal 2.80 \nNER States  \nArunachal Pradesh 0.21 \nAssam 3.50 \nManipur 0.40 \nMeghalaya 0.29 \nMizoram 0.11 \nNagaland 0.39 \nSikkim 0.16 \nTripura 0.26 \nTotal 100.00 \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nFoodgrain allocation for drought relief during 2015 -16 and 2016 -17 with reference to Lok Sabha \nQuestion No.404 raised by S/Shri D.K.Suresh, Prof. Saugata  Roy, Shivkumar Udasi and Dr.Majoj \nRajoria due for answer on 19.7.2016. \nStates Quantity Allocated (in tons) \n Rice Wheat Total \nMaharashtra 163000 244000 407000 \nAndhra Pradesh 102312 0 102312 \nChattisgarh 82000 0 82000 \nGujarat 8000 48000 56000 \nJharkhand 171103 21844 192947 \nKarnataka 2577 2556 5133 \nMadhya Pradesh 24000 72000 96000 \nTelangana 250292 0 250292 \nRajasthan 0 1138200 1138200 \nUttar Pradesh 28073 22961 51034 \nTotal 831357 1549561 2380918 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-III \nAssistance Approved from National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF) for Drought and Hailstorm \nwith reference to Lok Sabha  Question No.404 raised by S/Shri D.K.Suresh, Prof. Saugata Roy, \nShivkumar Udasi and Dr.Majoj Rajoria due for answer on 19.7.2016. \n(Rs. in crore) \nDuring – 2015-16  \nS.No. State Calamity Amount approved \n1. Karnataka Drought \nDrought-R \n1540.20 \n723.23 \n2. Chhattisgarh Drought 1276.25 \n3. Madhya Pradesh Drought 2032.68 \n4. Maharashtra Drought-K \nDrought-K -Supplementary \nDrought-R \n3049.36 \n# \n# \n5. Odisha Drought 815.00 \n6. Telangana Drought 791.21 \n7. Uttar Pradesh Drought \nDrought-R \n1304.52 \n# \n8. Andhra Pradesh Drought 433.77 \n9. Jharkhand Drought 336.94 \n10. Rajasthan Drought \nHailstorm \n1193.41 \n# \n11. Uttarakhand Drought-R # \nR:Rabi \n#: Proposals are at various stages of processing. \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_416": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n416\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n             \nSHORTAGE \nOF STORAGE FACILITY\n \n      \n \n416. \n \nSHRI RAHUL KASWAN: \n \nSHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nonion and potato farmers across the country are distressed due to fall in \nprices, lack of storage facilities and lack of food processing units; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, whether the Government proposes to build godowns to enhance the \ncapacity for storing onion and po\ntato in various States of the country including \nMaharashtra, if so, the details thereof, State\n-\nwise;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether farmers are compelled to sell off onion at the rate of Re. 1 per kilogram \nowing to shortage of storage facility and several farmers did not e\nven harvest onion \nfrom their fields; \n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the reaction of the Government thereto; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to increase storage capacity for onion and \npotato in various States of the country?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a):   \nWholesale and retail prices of Onion this year are generally lower than that of the \nlast year in almost \nall major markets including Maharashtra. On the other hand, potato \nfarmers are getting higher prices this year for their produce.\n \n \nThe total cold storage capacity in the country is around 34 million MT, out of \nwhich about 75% is used for storage of potato.\n \nOnion usually stored in farm proximate \nwell ventilated conventional storages. Financial assistance for setting up food \nprocessing units are provided by Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MOFPI). MIDH \nalso provides assistance for establishment of proc\nessing units under the sub \ncomponent of Horticulture Mission for North Eastern and Himalayan States (HMNEH).  \n \n \n Contd…2/ -  \n\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(b)\n:\n \nGovernment, through various schemes/mission, is providing financial assistance \nfor creation of additional cold storage/co\nld chain capacities in the country including \nMaharashtra. As on 31.03.2016, about 34 million MT capacity of Cold storage/cold \nchain infrastructure have been assisted/ created in various parts of the country. State \nwise cold storage capacity created is anne\nxed.\n \n \n(c) & (d)\n:\n \nAverage modal price of onion in Maharashtra has been above Rs. 670/ Qtl \nand All India average has been above Rs. 1130/Qtl since January 2016 (Source: \nAGMARKNET).\n \n \nDAC\n \n&\n \nFW implements Market Intervention Scheme (MIS) for procurement of \nperi\nshable commodities to protect the growers of these commodities from making \ndistress sale in the event of a bumper crop during the peak arrival period when the \nprices tend to fall below economic levels and cost of production.  Under the scheme, in \naccordanc\ne with MIS guidelines, a pre\n-\ndetermined quantity at the fixed Market \nIntervention Price (MIP) is procured by the agencies designated by the state \ngovernment for a fixed period or till the prices are stabilized above the Market \nIntervention Price (MIP) whic\nhever is earlier.  The area of operation is restricted to the \nconcerned state only.\n \n \nRecently, DAC\n \n&\n \nFW forwarded the request to various onion producing States \nincluding Maharashtra to send the proposal for procurement of onion under MIS.  \nHowever, no prop\nosal has been received from these State Governments so far.\n \n \n(e)\n:\n \nThe Government is implementing the following schemes/missions for increasing \nstorage capacity of perishables including onion and potato in the country.\n \n \n(i)\n \nMission for Integrated Developmen\nt of Horticulture (MIDH)  of  DAC&FW\n \n(ii)\n \nIntegr\nated cold chain Scheme of MOFPI\n \n \n(iii)\n \nScheme of Agricultural Processed Food Products Export Development Authority \n(APEDA) under ministry of Commerce\n \n(iv)\n \nScheme\n \nof National Cooperative Development Corporation (NCDC) under DAC \n&FW\n \n(v)\n \nGrameen Bhandaran Yojana of DAC&FW\n \n \nUnder these schemes, financial assistance in the form of grants in aid are \nprovided to farmers/entrepreneurs for setting up cold storages/cold cha\nin infrastructure. \nUnder MIDH financial assistance for low cost onion storage structure is also provided.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \nState wise Cold Storage capacity as on 31.03.2016\n \n \nS. No.\n \nName of the State\n \nNo.\n \nCapacity (MT)\n \n1\n \nAndaman & Nicobar Islands (UT)\n \n2\n \n210\n \n2\n \nAndhra Pradesh & Telangana\n \n426\n \n1729286\n \n3\n \nArunachal Pradesh\n \n1\n \n5000\n \n4\n \nAssam\n \n35\n \n152706\n \n5\n \nBihar\n \n305\n \n1416095\n \n6\n \nChandigarh (UT)\n \n7\n \n12462\n \n7\n \nChhattisgarh\n \n98\n \n484557\n \n8\n \nDelhi\n \n97\n \n129857\n \n9\n \nGoa\n \n29\n \n7705\n \n10\n \nGujarat\n \n692\n \n2570973\n \n11\n \nHaryana\n \n318\n \n695795\n \n12\n \nHimachal Pradesh\n \n53\n \n105726\n \n13\n \nJammu & Kashmir\n \n33\n \n100976\n \n14\n \nJharkhand\n \n57\n \n226680\n \n15\n \nKarnataka\n \n193\n \n548001\n \n16\n \nKerala\n \n196\n \n78105\n \n17\n \nLakshadweep (UT)\n \n1\n \n15\n \n18\n \nMadhya Pradesh\n \n294\n \n1253715\n \n19\n \nMaharashtra\n \n575\n \n881860\n \n20\n \nManipur\n \n1\n \n3000\n \n21\n \nMeghalaya\n \n4\n \n8200\n \n22\n \nMizoram\n \n3\n \n4471\n \n23\n \nNagaland\n \n2\n \n6150\n \n24\n \nOrissa\n \n167\n \n523139\n \n25\n \nPondicherry (UT)\n \n3\n \n85\n \n26\n \nPunjab\n \n655\n \n2152003\n \n27\n \nRajasthan\n \n159\n \n521387\n \n28\n \nSikkim\n \n2\n \n2100\n \n29\n \nTamil Nadu\n \n168\n \n316583\n \n30\n \nTripura\n \n14\n \n45477\n \n31\n \nUttar Pradesh\n \n2250\n \n13978608\n \n32\n \nUttrakhand\n \n44\n \n148921\n \n33\n \nWest Bengal\n \n511\n \n5940511\n \n \n \n \n \n7395\n \n34050359\n \n(Source: Directorate of Marketing and Inspection (DMI) upto 2009, National Horticulture Board \n (NHB), National Horticulture Mission (NHM) & Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI))  \n\n \n**********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_417": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n417\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n              \nSUBSIDY \nTO FARMERS\n \n      \n \n417. \n \nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE: \n \nSHRIMATI BHAVANA PUNDALIKRAO GAWALI PATIL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe\n \nnumber of farmers in the country at present, State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether a majority of small farmers are indebtedness in the country and if so, the \ndetails thereof along with the efforts being made by the Government to bail them out \nfrom debt; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwheth\ner the Government has formulated any policy for extending benefits of \nsubsidy and incentives to the farmers for cultivation of crops and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to constitute a commission to suggest ways \nand m\neans of raising farm income on the line of pay commission in the service sector \nand if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n   \nAs per the \nSituation Assessment Survey (SAS) of Agricultural Households\n, \nconducted\n \nby \nNational Sample Survey Office (NSSO\n) \nduring \nthe agricultural year July \n2012 to June 2013,\n \nthe \nestimated \nnumber of rural \nagricultural households\n \nin the country \nwas\n \n90.2 million\n. Out of this,\n \nabout 47 million households, i.e., \n5\n2\n \npercent\n,\n \nwere \nestimated to be under \ndebt\n \nfrom various sources\n. \nState\n-\nwise details are at Annexure\n-\nI. \n\nAbout 56 per \ncent of the small agricultural households (land holdings of 1\n-\n2 hectare) \nwere estimated to be indebted during the survey period.\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \nGovernment of India is making efforts for increasing the coverage of institutional \ncredit in the agricultural s\ne\nctor. As against the agricultural\n \ncredit target of Rs. 8,50,000 \ncrore for 2015\n-\n16, a total of Rs. 8,77,224 crore (provisional) has been advanced through \ncommercial banks, cooperative banks and regional rural banks. Further, an interest \nsubvention scheme i\ns under implementation since 2006\n-\n07. Under the Scheme, the \nfarmers who repay their crop loans as per the repayment schedule fixed by the banks \nenjoy 3 per cent interest subvention. Thus, making their effective interest rate for crop \nloan 4 per cent per an\nnum. \nIn order to discourage distress sale by farmers and to \nencourage them to store their produce, the benefit of interest subvention scheme has \nbeen extended to small and marginal farmers having Kisan Credit Card for a further \nperiod of upto six months, p\nost\n-\nharvest on the same rate as available to crop loan \nagainst negotiable warehouse receipts. \n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nThe existing Schemes of the \nGovernment\n \nhave provisions \nfor subsidies on \nvarious agricultural inputs such as, seeds, fertilizers, machinery and equipments e\ntc., to \nincentivize farmers in the country for cultivation of crops. \nGovernment also fixes \nMinimum Support Prices (MSP) for various agricultural crops, as an instrument to \nincentivize farmers to bring more area under cultivation of crops and to invest on \ni\nncreasing the productivity. Details of MSP announced during the last five years are \ngiven in Annexure\n-\nII.\n \n \n(d) No, Madam.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \nState\n-\nwise e\nstimated number of indebted Agricultural H