    "ls|LS16_S9_U_421": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 421 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19/07/2016 \n \n \nSETTING UP OF KVKs \n \n \n421. DR. KAMBHAMPATI HARIBABU: \n  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k ,oa fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has received a proposal to set up Krishi Vigyan Kendra in \nVisakhapatnam district of Andhra Pradesh; and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along  with the time by which the Krishi Vigyan Kendra is \nlikely to be set up? \n \n \nA N S W E R \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k ,oa fdlku dY;k.k ea=ky; esa jkT; ea=h \n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n \n(a) & (b): The Indian Coun cil of Agricultural Research has already set up a Krishi \nVigyan Kendra (KVK) in Visakhapatnam district of Andhra Pradesh. The Government has \nalso approved for establishment of one additional KVK in Visakhapatnam district during \n12th Plan. For this purpose,  one proposal has been received from Acharya N G Ranga \nAgricultural University (ANGRAU), Guntur, Andhra Pradesh. The Site Selection \nCommittee has identified the site for setting up of the additional KVK in Visakhapatnam \ndistrict. \n \n \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_422": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 422 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 19.07.2016 \n \nSPV for revival of closed units \n \n422.   ADV. NARENDRA KESHAV SAWAIKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n(a)  whether the Government has recently created any Special Purpose Vehicle  (SPV) \nfor revival of closed down urea plants of Hindustan Fertilizer  Corporation  (HFC) and \nFertilizer Corporation of India (FCI); \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the details of the financial burden on the Government for reviving these sick  units \nand sources from where it is proposed to be funded; and \n(d)  the time by which these plants are expected to earn profit? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS, SHIPPING, \nROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n(a) & (b):  Yes, Madam. The Union Cabinet in its meeting held on 13.07.2016 has approved to \nrevive three defunct fertilizer units, namely Gora khpur, Sindri units of the Fertilizer Corporation \nof India Limited and Barauni unit of Hindustan Fertilizer Corporation Limited, by means of \nSpecial Purpose Vehicle (SPV) of Public Sector Units (PSUs) namely, National Thermal Power \nCorporation (NTPC), Coal  India Limited (CIL), Indian Oil Corporation Limited (IOCL) and \nFCIL/HFCL, through nomination route. \n(c): There is no financial burden on the Government for reviving these units. All of these \nunits are to be revived subject to non -recourse to Government funding. Funding to revive these \nunits will be made by the PSUs constituting the special purpose vehicle (SPV). \n(d): No time frame has been decided at this stage. \n**********  \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_423": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 423 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nEnvironmental Clearance \n \n423.   DR. C. GOPALAKRISHNAN:  \nSHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA: \n \nWill the Minister of  ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether the Government has taken any initiative to improve environmental clearance \nprocess for infrastructure in the country and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the number and details of environment al clearance projects taken up by the Government \nduring each of the last three years and the current year; and \n(c) the number and details of projects pending for clearance during the said period? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change has taken various \ninitiatives to make the environmental clearance process more transparent and \nexpeditious, without compromising the integrity \nof the environment. The initiatives  \ninter alia  include us e of Information Communication Technology for environment \nclearance process, amendment of Environment Impact Assessment Notification, 2006 \ndelegating more powers to State  Level Environment Impact Assessment Authorities \n(SEIAAs), standardization of  Terms of Reference for preparation of Environment \nImpact Assessment Report and Environment Management Plan,  laying down \ntimeline for finalisation of T erms of Reference in 30 days, e xemption of highway \nprojects in border States from scoping requirement and linear projects in border \nStates from the requirement of public hearing subject to suitable conditions , \nexemption of construction of school s, college s, hostels for educational  institutions \nfrom the requirement of obtaining prior E nvironmental Clearance, delegation of the \npower to grant environmental clearance for mining of minor minerals (including sand \nmining) for 5 ha. of individual lease and 25 ha. in cluster, to D istrict Level \nEnvironment Impact Assessment Authorities (DEIAAs), etc. \n \n(b)  A total of 2306 projects were received in the Ministry during the last three years and \nthe current year.  The details are enclosed at Annexure-I. \n \n(c) A total of 432  projects are pending for environmental clearance  as on 30.6. 2016.  \nThe details are enclosed at Annexure-II. \n \n***** \n\n \n  \n\n  \n\nANNEXURE-I  referred in the statement referred to the Reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 423 due for \nreply on 19.7.2016 regarding Environmental Clearance raised by Dr. C. Gopalakrishnan and  Shri  Rabindra \nKumar Jena, Hon’ble Members of Parliament \n \nProject received for Environment Clearance during last three years including current year till 15.07.2016  \n \n Industry Thermal \nRiver \nValley & \nHydro- \nElectric \nMining \n(Coal) \nMining \n(Non- Coal) Infrastructure \nNuclear, \nDefence \n& \nOthers \nTotal \n2013 155 27 20 44 140 171 12 569 \n2014 154 14 13 44 94 310 14 643 \n2015 260 18 14 61 216 225 07 801 \n2016 \n (upto \n15.7.2016) \n116 09 4 09 67 82 06 293 \nTotal 685 68 51 158 517 788 39 2306 \n\nANNEXURE-II  referred in the statement referred to the Reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 423 \ndue for reply on 19.7.2016 regarding Environmental Clearance raised by Dr. C. Gopalakrishnan and                           \nShri  Rabindra Kumar Jena, Hon’ble Members of Parliament \n \n \nProjects Pending for Grant of Environment Clearance as on 30.06.2016 \n \n Industry Thermal \nRiver \nValley & \nHydro- \nElectric \nMining \n(Coal) \nMining \n(Non- Coal) Infrastructure \nNuclear, \nDefence \n& \nOthers \nTotal \nTotal 170 09 22 25 129 63 14 432 \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_424": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n424\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n               \nBENEFITS\n \nOF AGRICULTURAL SCHEMES\n \n \n424. \n \nSHRI NANA PATOLE: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to present a separate budget for agriculture to \nsolve the problems of this sector as 70 per cent of the country's population is dependent \nupon agriculture for livelihood and only 33 per cent land has the access to the irriga\ntion \nfacilities and rest is dependent on rains; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe time limit fixed by the Government for availing of benefits by the farmers from \nIrrigation Scheme, Crop Insurance Scheme and various other agriculture related \nsch\nemes; and \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe details of arrangements made/ steps taken by the Government for providing \nproper platform for \nredress\nsal of agriculture related problems being faced by the \nfarmers?\n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARME\nRS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a):   \n \nNo, Madam.\n \n \n(b)  \n \nDoes not arise.\n \n \n(c)\n:\n \nTo ensure early settlement of claims of farmers under Pradhan Mantri Fasal \nBima\n \nYojana (PMFBY), a defined timeline for every activity like enrolment of farmers, \nsubmission of yield/weather data, processing of claims by company and payment of \nclaims etc. has been defined in the Operational Guidelines of the scheme. \n \n\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \nA\ngriculture is a State subject and most of the schemes of Ministry of Agriculture \nand Farmers Welfare are being implemented by the State Government agencies.  \nFarmers can avail benefits of these schemes as per provisions of guidelines of the \nscheme and as p\ner  perceived need/requirement.\n \n \n(d)\n:\n \nApart from the various platforms available to the farmers for getting replies to \ntheir queries  and grievances relating to agricultural activities by the State Governments \nand other agencies, the public grievance redre\nssal mechanism of the Central \nGovernment enabled by the Department of Administrative Reforms & Public Grievances \nunder the Ministry of Personnel, Public  Grievances and Pensions coordinates receipt \nand disposal of various grievances of the public, includin\ng relating to the agriculture, \nthrough the administrative Ministries/Departments. Under this, every \nMinistry/Department of Central Government, including all the Departments in the \nMinistry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare, appoints a nodal officer for ha\nndling such \ngrievances and replying directly to the petitioners.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n**********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_425": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n425\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \n              \nMSP FOR KHARIF \nCROPS\n \n      \n \n425. \n \nSHRIMATI POONAMBEN MAADAM: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether the Government has increased the Minimum Support Price (MSP) on \nKharif\n \ncrops including paddy and pulses, if so, the details thereof, crop\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government has ensured 100% procurement of pulses at MSP like \npaddy and wheat, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government is aware that MSP for \npulses was up by almost 50% \nduring the last five years but production failed to increase and if so, the steps taken by \nthe Government to mitigate the challenge with a hike in MSP?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  \nWELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \nGovernment has increased the Minimum Support Prices (MSPs) for all Kharif \nCrops including paddy and pulses of 2016\n-\n17 season\n. Details of increase in MSP for \nkharif crops for 2016\n-\n17 season including paddy and pulses is given at \nAnnexure.\n \n \n(b):\n \nTo ensure procurement of pulses, Government has designated Food Corporation \nof India (FCI) as central nodal agency. The National Agricul\ntural Cooperative Marketing \nFederation of India limited (NAFED), National Cooperative Consumers’ Federation \n(NCCF), Central Warehousing Corporation (CWC) and Small Farmers Agri\n-\nBusiness \nConsortium (SFAC) will supplement efforts of FCI for price support ope\nrations for \npulses.\n \n \n \nThe objective of the Government’s price policy is to ensure remunerative prices \nto farmers by offering to procure their produce at MSP. However, farmers are free to sell \ntheir produce to Government procurement agencies or in the open \nmarket as is \nadvantageous to them.\n \n \n(c):\n \nMSP is one of the incentives for farmers to increase production of pulses. In \naddition, the pr\noduction of pulses depends upon several factors which \ninter alia \ninclude \nweather condition, quality of seed, technologica\nl development, etc. During 2016\n-\n17 \nGovernment has increased the Minimum Support Prices (MSPs) of pulses. Further, to \nincentivize cultivation of pulses in the country, Government has announced a bonus of \nRs.425/\n-\n \nper quintal for kharif pulses, namely Arhar \n(Tur), Urad and Moong, in addition, \nGo\nvernment is implementing the National Food Security Mission (NFSM) \n–\n \nPulses since \n2008\n-\n09 through State Governments. NFSM\n-\nPulses \nis being implemented in 622 \n\nDistricts of 27 States including all Districts of North\n-\nEaste\nrn and hill States for promotion \nof pulses.\n \n \nAnnexure\n \n \n \nMinimum Support Price for Kharif crops for 2016\n-\n17 season including paddy and \npulses\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n(in Rs.per quintal)\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nMSP increase in 2016\n-\n17 \nover 2015\n-\n16\n \nSl.No.\n \nCommodity\n \nVariety\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n2016\n-\n17\n \nAbsolute\n \nIn %\n \n \nKHARIF CROPS\n \n \n \n \n \n \n1\n \nPADDY\n \nCommon\n \n1410\n \n1470\n \n60\n \n4.26\n \n \n \nGrade ‘A’\n \n1450\n \n1510\n \n60\n \n4.14\n \n2\n \nJOWAR\n \nHybrid\n \n1570\n \n1625\n \n55\n \n3.50\n \n \n \nMaldandi\n \n1590\n \n1650\n \n60\n \n3.77\n \n3\n \nBAJRA\n \n \n1275\n \n1330\n \n55\n \n4.31\n \n4\n \nMAIZE\n \n \n1325\n \n1365\n \n40\n \n3.02\n \n5\n \nRAGI\n \n \n1650\n \n1725\n \n75\n \n4.55\n \n6\n \nARHAR \n(Tur)\n \n \n4625^\n \n5050^^\n \n425\n \n9.19\n \n7\n \nMOONG\n \n \n4850^\n \n5225^^\n \n375\n \n7.73\n \n8\n \nURAD\n \n \n4625^\n \n5000^^\n \n375\n \n8.11\n \n9\n \nCOTTON\n \nMedium\n \nStaple\n \n3800\n \n3860\n \n60\n \n1.58\n \n \n \nLong \nStaple\n \n4100\n \n4160\n \n60\n \n1.46\n \n10\n \nGROUNDNUT \nIN SHELL\n \n \n4030\n \n4220*\n \n190\n \n4.71\n \n11\n \nSUNFLOWER \nSEED\n \n \n3800\n \n3950*\n \n150\n \n3.95\n \n12\n \nSOYABEEN\n \nBlack\n \n-\n \n-\n \n \n \n \n \nYellow$$\n \n2600\n \n2775*\n \n175\n \n6.73\n \n13\n \nSESAMUM\n \n \n4700\n \n5000^\n \n300\n \n6.38\n \n1\n4\n \nNIGERSEDD\n \n \n3650\n \n3825*\n \n175\n \n4.79\n \n \n$$  MSP of soyabean yellow is also applicable to black variety.\n \n \n*  Including bonus of Rs.100 per quintal\n \n^  Including\n \nbonus of Rs.200 per quintal\n \n^^ Including bonus of Rs.425 per quintal\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n**********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_426": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 426 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 19.07.2016 \n \nrevival of fertilizer plants \n \n426  SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:  \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n (a)  whether the Government is considering to collaborate with the Central Public  Sector \nUndertakings for revival of fertilizer plants in the country;  \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the details of fertilizer plants presently under consideration of the  Government for \nrevival under such collaboration; and  \n(d)  the steps taken for revival of those plants which are left out? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & F ERTILIZERS, SHIPPING, \nROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n(a) to (d):  The Union Cabinet in its meeting held on 13.07.2016 has approved to revive three \ndefunct fertilizer units, namely Gorakhpur, Sindri  units of the Fertilizer Corporation of India \nLimited (FCIL) and Barauni unit of Hindustan Fertilizer Corporation Limited(HFCL), by means of \nSpecial Purpose Vehicle of Public Sector Units (PSUs) namely, National Thermal Power \nCorporation, Coal India Limited (CIL), Indian Oil Corporation Limited and FCIL/HFCL, through \nnomination route.  \n.  Talcher is being revived by Rashtriya Chemicals & Fertilizers Limited, Gail (India) Ltd, \nCIL and FCIL  and Ramagundam is  being revived by National Fertilizers Limited, Engineers \nIndia Limited and FCIL. \n \n********* \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_427": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 427 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nIMPLEMENTATION OF NFSA \n427.  DR. K. GOPAL: \nKUMARI SHOBHA KARANDLAJE: \nSHRI G. HARI: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken the final decision to implement the \nNational Food S ecurity Act (NFSA) immediately  and to bring the whole country \nunder its ambit, if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether some States are yet to identify the beneficiaries under the said \nAct, if so, the details thereof and the corrective steps proposed in this regard \nindicating the number and percentage of cards digitised and linked to Aadhaar; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has reviewed the implementation of the scheme in \nthe States, if so, the details and the outcome thereof; and \n \n(d)  the funds allocated, released and utilised under the Act by the Union and \nthe State Governments during the last three years, State-wise? \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n (a) to (c): Under the National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA), which came into \nforce with effect from 05.07.2013, main responsibility for implementation, which \ninter alia includes identification of eligible households, issuing ration cards to \nthem, distribution of foodgrain entitlem ents to eligible households through fair \nprice shops (FPS), setting up effective grievance redressal mechanism, necessary \nstrengthening of Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS), rests with the \nStates/UTs. \n \nImplementation of the Act is reviewed on regular basis through meetings \nwith States/UTs at various levels. As a result of regular review, implementation of \nthe Act has started in all the States/UTs except Kerala and Tamil Nadu. Recently, \na meeting of Food Ministers and Food Secretaries of States/UTs was held on \n21.05.2016 at New Delhi, to review implementation of NFSA and progress in end-\nto-end computerization, wherein Governments of Kerala and Tamil Nadu were \nrequested to expedite implementation of NFSA.  \n \n          Digitization of ration cards has been completed in all the States/UTs and \n60% of digitized ration cards have been seeded with Aadhaar. \n \n(d): For providing central assistance to States/UTs for meeting expenditure \ntowards intra-state movement and handling of foodgrains and FPS dealers margin \nunder NFSA, the Food Security (Assistance to State Governments) Rules, 2015 \nwas notified in August, 2015 and for the first time budget allocation was made in \n2015-16.  The amount of Rs. 279.01 lakh provided in Revised Estimates (2015- 16) \nwas fully released.   During the current year, an allocation of Rs. 250000 lakh has \nbeen made, out of which Rs. 86341.63 lakh has been released/sanctioned for \nrelease.  State/UT-wise details are at Annexure. \n**** \n\n \nANNEXURE \nANNEURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY OF PART (d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NUMBER 427 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 19.07.2016. \nCentral assistance released/sanctioned for release to States/UTs for \nmeeting expenditure towards intra- State movement & handling of \nfoodgrains and Fair Price Shop Dealers’ margin. \n2015-16 \n \nS.No. Name of the States/UTs Amount (in lakh) \n1 Andaman & Nicobar 4.55 \n2 Chandigarh 31.11 \n3 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 3.79 \n4 Daman & Diu 12.63 \n5 Delhi 35.03 \n6 Goa 56.4 \n7 Lakshadweep 13.89 \n8 Mizoram 31.72 \n9 Sikkim 89.89 \n  Total 279.01 \n   \n2016-17 \n1 Bihar 24007.21 \n2 Assam 4500.00 \n3 Tripura 800.00 \n4 Karnataka 16400.00 \n5 Maharashtra 15000.00 \n6 Rajasthan 25634.42 \n  Total 86341.63 \n \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_428": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 428 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nFLOUR PRICES \n \n428. SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE: \n(OIH) \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the steep increase in the prices of flour by the \nmills and has received complaints in this regard during the last three years;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n© the details of the action taken or proposed to be taken by the Government to rein in \nsuch flour mills and control the price of flour? \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) & (b) :  According to Wholesale Price Index (WPI) data brought out by Department of \nIndustrial Policy and Promoti on (DIPP), the prices of wheat flour have increased by 7.57% \nover the year in June, 2016 as against an increase of 5.54% recorded during May, 2016. No \ncomplaints with regard to increase in price of flour by mills have been received by this \nDepartment.  \n \n(c)      : Government through Food Corporation of India sells wheat at pre -determined \nprices in the open market from time to time under Open Market Sale Scheme (Domestic) to \nenhance the supply of wheat especially during the lean season to moderate open marke t prices \nin the deficit regions. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_429": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 429 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nABUSE OF DRUGS BY STUDENTS \n \n429.  SHRI MULLAPPALLY RAMACHANDRAN\": \nSHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware  that the use of drugs among the \nstudents is on the rise in the NCT of Delhi and if so,  the details thereof \nand the number of such  cases reported during the last twelve months in \nthe National Capital Territory of Delhi; \n \n(b) whether there are reports regarding  sale, purchase and easy \navailability of  drugs, narcotic and psychotropic  substances near the \nschools and educational  institutions and operation of drug mafia in  the \nNCT of Delhi; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the  corrective action taken by the \nGovernment and the Social Justice Department in this regard; and \n \n(d) the other measures taken by the  Government to stop availability of \nsuch substances near schools and educational institutions in the NCT of \nDelhi? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to (c):  The Government is concerned about the use of drugs by \nstudents in the NCT of Delhi as well as in other parts  of the country. \nHowever, no specific reports have been received by the Government \nregarding   rise in the use of drugs by students in the NCT of Delhi or the \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 429 FOR 19.07.2016 \n easy availability of drugs, narcotics and psychotropi c substances near \nschools and educational institutions. While the Ministry of Social Justice \nand Empowerment is actively engaged in drug -de-addiction and \nrehabilitation of ad dicts for drug demand reduction;  on the enforcement \nside, the total number of cases filed and number of persons arrested by \nvarious agencies in the NCT of Delhi is as under: \n \nParticulars 2014 2015 \nCases 361 357 \nArrests 434 417 \n \n(d):  Measures to stop availability of drugs include  awareness  \nprogrammes in schools and colleges, .effective surveillance  over the \nactivities of criminals involved in drug trafficking, integrated police \nchecking, consisting of Local Police, PCR and Traffic Police  of such \npeople and taking stringent legal action under appropriate sections of \nlaw again st persons involved in drug trafficking . Besides, the  National \nPolicy on Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances provides strict \nguidelines to prevent  sale of drugs to school children. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_430": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.430 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nReport on Air Quality \n \n430. SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  whether the report of the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) on air quality in 15 \ncities shows that most of the cities  i n the country are breaching the National annual \naverage standard margin of safety; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; city-wise;  \n(c)  whether the CPCB and State Pollution Control Boards are unable to check air  pollution \nin the country; \n(d)  if so, the reasons therefor; and \n(e)  the details of fresh  steps being taken by the Government to ensure that people living in \ncities get pollution free air? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) & (b)  A r eport on Ambient Air Quality Status in 15 Major Cities of India has been \nsubmitted by Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) in Original Application No. 21 of \n2014 in the matter of Vardhaman Kaushik Vs. Union of India & O thers to National Green \nTribunal. The analysis of the monitored parameters of SO\n2, NO2 and PM10, in the 15 cities \ncovering 8 states  during 2011 to 2015 indicates that SO 2 levels were very low in all the 15 \ncities ranged between 3 µg/m³ to 32 µg/m³ against the National Annual Average Standard of \n50 µg/m³. The NO2 levels were within the prescribed National Ambient Air Quality Standard \n(NAAQS) in the cities except Pune  and K olkata during most of the years and f luctuating \ntrends of NO 2 in the range of 13µg/m³ to 70µg/m³ was observed in the five years. T he \nParticulate Matter (PM10) levels were observed fluctuating trend in all the fifteen (15)  cities. \nThe ambient air quality status with respect to  annual averages for SO 2, NO 2 and PM 10 for \nfive years (2011-2015) is given at Annexure. \n \n(c)  No, Sir. \n \n(d) Does not arise in view of answer at (c) above. \n \n(e)  The major steps being taken by the Government to ensure that people living in cities get \npollution free air inter alia include the following:- \n\n \n(i) Notification of National Ambient Air Quality Standards; \n(ii) Formulation of environmental regulations / statutes; \n(iii) Setting up of monitoring network for assessment of ambient air quality; \n(iv) Introduction of cleaner / alternate fuels like gaseous fuel (CNG, LPG etc.), ethanol \nblend etc.; \n(v) Promotion of cleaner production processes. \n(vi) Launching of National Air Quality index by the Prime Minister in April, 2015; \n(vii) Implementation of Bharat Stage IV (BS -IV) norms in 63 selected cities and \nuniversalization of BS-IV by 2017; \n(viii) Decision taken to leapfrog directly from BS -IV to BS -VI fuel standards by 1st  \nApril, 2020; \n(ix) Taxing polluting vehicles and incentivizing hybrid and electric vehicles; \n(x) Comprehensive amendments to various Waste Management Rules including \nMunicipal Solid Waste, Plastic Waste, Hazardous Waste, Bio -medical Waste and \nElectronic Waste notified; \n(xi) Notification of Construction and Demolition Waste Management Rules; \n(xii) Ban on burning of leaves, biomass, municipal solid waste; \n(xiii) Promotion of public transport network of metro, buses, e -rickshaws and \npromotion of car pooling, Pollution Under Control, l ane discipline, vehicle \nmaintenance; \n(xiv) Revision of existing environmental standards and formulation of new standards \nfor prevention and control of pollution from industries; \n(xv) Regular co -ordination meetings at official and ministerial level with Delhi and \nother State Governments within the NCR;  \n(xvi) Issuance of directions under Section 5 of Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 and \nunder Section 18(1)(b) of Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1974 \nand Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, 1981; \n(xvii) Installation of on-line continuous (24x7) monitoring devices by major industries. \n \n*** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARA (a) & (b ) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 430  DUE FOR REPLY ON 1 9.07.2016 REGARDING \n‘REPORT ON AIR QUALITY ’ BY SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA , HON'BLE \nMEMBER OF PARLIAMENT  \n \nAir quality status of 15 cities for 2011-2015 \n(Annual average in µg/m3) \n \nS. \nNo. \nCity \n \nState \n \n2011 2012 2013 2014 2015 \nSO2 NO2 PM10 SO2 NO2 PM10 SO2 NO2 PM10 SO2 NO2 PM10 SO2 NO2 PM10 \n1.  Bihar Patna 5 47* 174* 6 36 166* - - - - - - - - - \n \n2.  Karnataka Bangaluru 16 29 94* 14 28 121* 13 26 113* 13 30 140* 5 20 131* \n \n3.  \nMaharashtra \nMumbai 5 35 119* 5 20 117* 3 13 117* 4 20 95* 3 23 90* \n \n4.  Nagpur 8 34 106* 10 32 103* 8 27 89* 10 25 93* 10 29 85* \n \n5.  Pune 32 58* 113* 22 45 92* 20 41* 88* 23 45* 92* 20 59* 96* \n \n6.  Tamilnadu Chennai 12 19 65* 12 21 57 14 22 75* 13 22 59 13 20 56 \n \n7.  Telangana Hyderabad 5 29 86* 4 28 79* 5 24 90* 5 24 98* 5 25 94* \n \n8.  \nPunjab \n \nLudhiana 11 28 221* 11 27 228* 11 26 204* 10 26 152* 11 27 139* \n \n9.  Amritsar 14 36 210* 15 39 202* 13 40 180* 14 42* 187* 12 34 169* \n \n10.  Jalandhar 12 26 142* 13 26 136* 13 27 164* 13 26 144* 13 25 137* \n \n11.  \nUttar \nPradesh \nLucknow 8 33 189* 8 32 211* 8 29 192* 8 28 175* 8 28 172* \n \n12.  Allahabad 6 23 260* 4 32 317* 5 29 235* 4 28 250* 3 28 249* \n \n13.  Kanpur 8 33 184* 8 34 215* 7 31 201* 5 34 199* 6 35 200* \n \n14.  Varanasi 17 20 127* 18 21 138* 19 28 145* 19 32 139* 19 36 174* \n \n15.  West \nBengal Kolkata 13 66* 115* 12 70* 135* 11 70* 159* 15 IA 107* 6 53* 108* \n \n \nNB. *Concentration exceeding NAAQS of 50 µg/m3 for SO\n2, 40 µg/m3 for NO 2, 60 µg/m3 for PM 10, for Residential/ industrial / \nother area. “-“data not available \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_431": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 431 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nASSISTANCE FOR CALAMITY RELIEF \n \n†431. SHRI SADASHIV LOKHANDE: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total loss of lives, property, cattle  and agricultural produce \nincurred due to natural calamities including floods in various parts of the \ncountry including Maharashtra  during each of the last  three years and \nthe current year, State-wise; and \n \n(b) the amount of assistance provided  by the Union Government to the \nState Governments including the grant of  assistance to farmers \nparticularly those belonging to backward and tribal regions? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a):  The details of the losses due to natural disasters reported by the \nState Governments including Maharashtra during the last three year and \ncurrent year are at Annexure-I. \n \n(b):  The concerned State Governments undertake relief activit ies \naccording to the magnitude of the ground situation, in the wake of \nnatural disasters from the State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF) already \nplaced at their disposal in accordance with Government of India  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 431 FOR 19.07.2016 \napproved items and norms without any discrimination. For calamity of a \nsevere nature, additional assistance is supplemented by Government of \nIndia from the National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF)  by following the \nlaid down procedure. This Ministry  does not maintain centrally  status of \nfarmers including those belonging to the backward and tribal regions -\nwise whom grant of assistance provided, since execution of relief \nactivities on the ground is responsibility of the State concerned.  \n \nState-wise details of allocation and releases form SDRF and NDRF during \nthe last three year and the 