    "ls|LS16_S9_U_435": "Government of India \nMinistry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution \nDepartment of Consumer Affairs \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.435 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nE-COMMERCE COMPLAINTS \n \n435. SHRIMATI RAKSHATAI KHADSE:  \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up a panel/committee to deal with the \ncomplaints of the consumers against companies trading on e -commerce platforms and if \nso, the details thereof indicating the composition of the said committee; and  \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to frame rules and regulations for consumer \nprotection on e-trading platforms and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nmiHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  \n¼Jh jke foykl ikloku½ \n \nTHE MINISTER OF  \nCONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a) & (b) : No Madam. There is no such proposal, at present.  \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_437": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 437 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nALLEGED MIGRATION OF HINDUS \n \n437.  SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether migration of Hindus due to  fear factor in Uttar Pradesh has \nbeen reported; \n \n(b) if so, the number of persons who have migrated and the details of the \nefforts made by the Government for their rehabilitation; \n \n(c) the action  taken by the Government  to check the same and punish \nthe persons responsible for this incident; \n \n(d) whether the Government has taken  special measures to protect the \nimmovable properties of the Hindus who have migrated; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof  alongwith the details of advisories issued by \nthe Union Government to Uttar Pradesh in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) to (e):  There were recent media reports about migration of \nHindus in Uttar Pradesh. A report in this regard has been received \nvery recently from the Government of Uttar Pradesh that would \nneed  examination. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_438": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.438 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nAfforestation in Hilly Areas \n \n \n438.     SHRI RAGHAV LAKHANPAL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has allocated/sanctioned funds for afforestation of hilly \nforest areas in the country;\n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof during each of the last three years, State/UT -wise including \nUttar Pradesh;  \n(c)  the total area proposed to be brought under forest cover under the said project,              \nState-wise; \n(d) whether the Government has any proposal  for setting up of Wind Mills under the said \nschemes; and  \n(e) the time by which the work of afforestation and setting up of Wind Mills is likely to \nbe completed? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) & (b)   The Ministry of Environment, Forests and Climate Change (MoEF & CC) is \nimplementing a centrally  sponsored “National Afforestation Programme (NAP)” scheme for \nregeneration and restocking of degraded forests in the country.  The scheme is implemented in \nparticipatory mode under the Joint Forest Management  by State Forest Development Agency \n(SFDA) at the State level, Forest Development Agency (FDA) at the forest division/district level \nand Joint Forest Management Committee (JFMC) at the village level. The Scheme is \nimplemented in 800 FDAs including 248 FDAs falling in hilly forest areas of the country.   \nUnder NAP, the grants are released to SFDAs and implementation is monitored at the state level. \nThe details of funds released to the States including Uttar Pradesh under NAP during the last \nthree years is given in Annexure.  \n \n \n(c)  National Forest policy  aims 33% forest cover in the country. The area f or affortestation \nunder NAP is san ctioned to States on receipt of Annual Plan of Operation (APO) and by \nconsidering their proposed demand, past performance and the Budget availability.  Afforestation \nunder NAP has contributed to the forest cover  of the country  as an area of about 21 lakhs \nhectares has been sanctioned for new plantations under NAP since its inception in 2000- 02 till \n2015-16 by releasing Rs. 3639.28 crores.   \n    \n(d)  No Sir.  There is no proposal under the NAP Scheme for setting up of Wind Mills. \n \n(e) NAP is an open ending scheme where the work of  plantation and maintenance activities \nare continuously going on. As fa r as completion of setting up of Wind Mills  under NAP is \nconcerned, the question does not arise.  \n \n******** \n\n \nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply of parts (a &  b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNo.438 on “Afforestation in Hilly Areas ” asked by Shri Raghav Lakhanpal for reply on \n19.07.2016. \n \nYearwise and Statewise Details of funds released under National Afforestation \nProgramme(NAP) for the last three years \n \nRs. crores \nS. No. State 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1 Andhra Pradesh  3.75 4.56 0.87 \n2 Bihar 12.84 7.00 5.01 \n3 Chhattisgarh 21.38 20.00 10.20 \n4 Gujarat  11.68 10.50 0.00 \n5 Haryana  17.94 11.00 8.54 \n6 Himachal Pradesh  2.61 0.73 0.72 \n7 Jammu & Kashmir  8.11 3.59 0.00 \n8 Jharkhand 9.02 8.60 0.00 \n9 Karnataka  9.26 21.35 0.00 \n10 Kerala 6.99 2.82 1.05 \n11 Madhya Pradesh  22.10 21.00 1.02 \n12 Maharashtra  32.33 35.00 9.45 \n13 Orissa  5.36 17.92 14.90 \n14 Punjab  2.00 1.87 9.49 \n15 Rajasthan  2.81 3.35 0.00 \n16 Tamil Nadu  3.21 4.25 0.75 \n17 Telangana 0.00 2.03 1.42 \n18 Uttar Pradesh  20.15 12.00 0.00 \n19 Uttarakhand  6.01 2.50 2.68 \n20 West Bengal 2.96 0.78 3.05 \n  Total (Other \nStates) \n200.49 190.85 69.15 \n  North Eastern \nstates \n     \n21 Arunachal Pradesh  0.00 0.15 0.00 \n22 Assam 2.99  - 2.56 \n23 Manipur  12.12 8.00 3.67 \n24 Meghalaya 4.50 3.61 1.35 \n25 Mizoram  11.94 15.00 10.17 \n26 Nagaland  9.82 11.00 0.00 \n27 Sikkim  3.77 6.00 1.52 \n28 Tripura 11.99 9.17 5.73 \n  Total (NE States) 57.13 52.93 25.00 \n  G. Total 257.62 243.78 94.16 \n     \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_439": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMNAL HUSBANDRY, DAIRYING & FISHERIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.439 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nGUIDELINES ON DEEP SEA FISHING \n \n439. SHRIMATI K. MARAGATHAM: \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE  कृषि और कृिक कल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) Whether the deep sea fishing guidelines mandate boats, that are above 15 meters overall length, \nengaged in near shore fishing just beyond the territorial wat ers of India, to obtain a letter of \npermission from the Government of India and voyage clearance from the Indian Coast Guard \nfor each fishing voyage; \n(b) if so the details thereof; \n(c) whether the new guidelines not only restrict the open access of the country’s f ishermen in the \nExclusive Economic Zone but also set a dangerous precedent of allowing foreign vessels of \nmore than 15 meters overall length to compete with our fishermen for resources which need  to \nbe conserved for utilization by the country’s fishermen;  \n(d) if so, the steps taken by the Government to protect the interest of the country’s fishermen; and \n(e) whether the Government has received a representation from the State Government of Tamil \nNadu in this regard, if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE: \n(SHRI SUDARSHAN BHAGAT) \n \n(a) & (b) Yes, Madam. As per the Guidelines for operation of Deep Sea F ishing Vessels in the \nIndian Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) issued on 12th November, 2014 a Letter of Permission \n(LOP) is required from the Department of Animal Husbandry, Dairying and Fisheries, Ministry \nof Agriculture & Farmers Welfare, Government of India for operating any fishing vessel(s) of \n15 meter overall length (OAL) and above or any resource specific vessel or any vessel fit for \ndeep sea fishing for their operation in Indian EE Z beyond 12 nautical miles . The LOP holder \nDSFVs are required to comply all the conditions stipulated under the said Guidelines dated 12 th \nNovember, 2014 which includes inspection of vessel(s) by nearest Coast Guard Authority \nbefore and after completion of each voyage. \n(c) No, Madam.  Any Indian citizen, Indian entrepreneur; Partnership Firm; Private Ltd. Company; \nPublic Ltd. Company; Joint Venture Company (with at least 51% Indian equity), Corporation \nand registered Cooperative Society may apply for obtaining LOP for deep -sea fishing in the \nIndian EEZ beyond 12 nautical miles. Whereas , foreign vessels are not allowed to fish within \nthe Exclusive Economic Zone of India. \n(d) Does not arise. \n(e) The State Government of Tamil Nadu vide its letter dated 03.5.2015 had raised  its concerns on \nthe Guidelines dated12th November, 2014. A Committee has been constituted on 28.7.2015 to \nsuggest draft of the National Policy on Marine Fisheries and also to review the Guidelines for \ndeep-sea fishing. \n******* ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_440": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n440\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nRASHTRIYA KRISHI VIKAS YOJANA \n \n      \n \n440. \n \nSHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nthe\n \nannual growth target fixed for agriculture sector during the financial years \n2015\n-\n16 and 2016\n-\n17 and the extent to which the targets have been achieved; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the State Governments have implemented the Rashtriya Krishi Vikas \nYojana (RKVY) as per \nthe guidelines of the scheme; \n \n \n(c) \n \nif so, whether the Government has played its role in checking and implementing \nthe schemes effectively at national level; \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Comptroller and Auditor General of India have drawn the attention of \nthe Gover\nnment towards certain drawbacks of the schemes in its State\n-\nwise review; \nand \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, whether the Government proposes to chalk out any effective policy for \nmeeting the annual growth target of the Agriculture sector?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE \n \nMINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \nto (c)\n:   \n \nRashtriya\n \nKrishi\n \nVikas\n \nYojana (RKVY) was launched during 2007\n-\n08 to \nprovide Additional Central \nAssistance to states to incentivize them to increase their \ninvestment in agriculture and allied sectors to achieve 4%  annual growth in this sector \nduring XI plan period. No specific annual growth target has been fixed for agriculture \nsector under the sche\nme during the particular financial years 2015\n-\n16 and 2016\n-\n17. As \nper the Provisional Estimates of Annual National Income 2015\n-\n16 and Quarterly \nEstimate of GDP released on 31.05.2016, the growth (over the previous year) in the \nGross Value Added (GVA) of agr\niculture and allied sector during 2015\n-\n16 is 1.2% at \n2011\n-\n12 prices. The scheme is being implemented in all states as per the guidelines of \nthe scheme and monitored through web\n-\nbased Management Information System. The \n\nstate\n-\nwise allocation and release of f\nunds since inception of the scheme is given at \nAnnexure.\n \nContd….2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(d) & (e): \n \nYes, Madam. Comptroller and Auditor General of India have reported \ncertain \nshortcomings\n \nin implementation of the scheme in the states during 2007\n-\n08 to \n2012\n-\n13. Government of India has already revised the scheme guidelines for \nimplementation from the financial year 2014\n-\n15 of the XII Plan to make implementation \nof the scheme more effective i\nn the states. As per the revised guidelines of the scheme, \nstates are required to constitute State Level Project Screening Committee (SLPSC) \nheaded by Agriculture Production Commissioner or any other officer nominated by Chief \nSecretary for screening RKVY \nproject proposals prepared by various departments of the \nstate for its suitability, its linkage to District Agriculture Plans (DAPs) and State \nAgriculture Plan (SAP) and its adherence to the RKVY guidelines before the projects \nare placed before the State L\nevel Sanctioning Committee (SLSC) headed by the Chief \nSecretary of the concerned states for approval.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement annexed in reply to the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 440 due for answer on 19.07.2016 \n                            \nStatement showing state\n-\nwise allocation and release of funds under RKVY from financial year 2007\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSl. \nNo\n \nName of the \nState/UT\n \n2007\n-\n08\n \n2008\n-\n09\n \n2009\n-\n10\n \n2010\n-\n11\n \n2011\n-\n12\n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation\n \nRelease  \n \nAllocation \n1\n \nAndhra Pradesh \n \n93.13\n \n61.08\n \n316.57\n \n297.17\n \n410.00\n \n410.00\n \n393.45\n \n432.29\n \n727.74\n \n734.20\n \n601.98\n2\n \nArunachal \nPradesh\n \n2.85\n \n1.90\n \n6.88\n \n0.00\n \n16.10\n \n15.98\n \n39.08\n \n28.95\n \n8.26\n \n10.68\n \n3\n \nAssam \n \n23.77\n \n0.00\n \n142.62\n \n144.12\n \n79.86\n \n79.86\n \n256.87\n \n216.87\n \n227.77\n \n227.77\n \n399.57\n4\n \nBihar\n \n64.02\n \n57.77\n \n148.54\n \n148.54\n \n110.79\n \n110.79\n \n380.94\n \n415.10\n \n506.82\n \n506.82\n \n724.01\n5\n \nChhatisgarh  \n \n60.54\n \n52.96\n \n116.48\n \n117.45\n \n131.78\n \n136.14\n \n461.00\n \n503.42\n \n230.57\n \n212.61\n \n581.12\n6\n \nGoa\n \n2.29\n \n1.70\n \n6.91\n \n0.00\n \n11.87\n \n0.00\n \n11.31\n \n7.07\n \n49.55\n \n24.78\n \n7\n \nGujarat\n \n53.71\n \n49.81\n \n243.39\n \n243.39\n \n386.19\n \n386.19\n \n353.45\n \n388.63\n \n515.48\n \n515.48\n \n586.87\n8\n \nHaryana\n \n23.12\n \n21.52\n \n74.00\n \n39.50\n \n112.77\n \n112.77\n \n204.74\n \n226.80\n \n168.92\n \n176.87\n \n199.49\n9\n \nHimachal \nPradesh\n \n17.39\n \n16.17\n \n15.11\n \n15.11\n \n33.02\n \n33.03\n \n94.85\n \n94.85\n \n99.93\n \n99.93\n \n10\n \nJammu & \nKashmir\n \n6.85\n \n0.00\n \n16.17\n \n1.20\n \n42.05\n \n42.85\n \n162.16\n \n96.42\n \n103.03\n \n63.03\n \n112.08\n11\n \nJharkhand \n \n61.66\n \n55.68\n \n58.62\n \n29.31\n \n70.13\n \n70.13\n \n160.96\n \n96.90\n \n168.56\n \n174.56\n \n241.55\n12\n \nKarnataka\n \n171.97\n \n154.30\n \n316.57\n \n314.14\n \n410.00\n \n410.00\n \n284.03\n \n284.03\n \n595.90\n \n595.90\n \n586.52\n13\n \nKerala\n \n61.41\n \n55.40\n \n60.11\n \n30.06\n \n110.92\n 