    "ls|LS16_S9_U_447": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 447 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nARRESTS BY NIA IN HYDERABAD \n \n447.   SHRIMATI KOTHAPALLI GEETHA : \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the National Investigation  Agency (NIA) has recently \narrested some  persons in Hyderabad who are alleged to  be part of a \nconspiracy to carry out terror  strikes at religious places to trigger \ncommunal riots in Hyderabad and  Bengaluru and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n \n(b) whether the Government is carrying  out searches from time to time \nto bust IS and other terrorist modules in the country and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n \n(a): The National Investigation Agency (NIA) has registered a case to \ninvestigate the allegations that some youth and their accomplices have \nentered into a criminal conspiracy to wage war against the Government \nof India and were collecting weapons and explosive  material to target \npublic places including religious sites etc., in various parts of the \ncountry.  NIA has so far arrested seven persons from Hyderabad in this \ncase.  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 447 FOR 19.07.2016 \n(b): In order to counter terror activities, t here exists a close and \neffective coordination between intelligence and security agencies at the \nCentre and the State l evels.  This has resulted in busting of several \nterrorist modules including ISIS/ISIL cadres, by the Security Agencies. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_448": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 448 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 19.07.2016 \n \nDiversion of subsidized fertilizers  \n \n \n448 SHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU: \n \n Will the Minister of CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether highly subsidized urea is being diverted for industrial use leading to  its \nshortage for agriculture, if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether the Government has made it mandatory for all domestic urea manufacturers to \nproduce and importers to import neem coated urea to be used for agriculture in order to \ncurb its illegal diversion, if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(b) the total amount of subsidy as well as foreign exchange likely to be saved as  a \nresult thereof?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE   MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS, SHIPPING, \nROAD TRANSPORT & HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI MANSUKH L. MANDAVIYA) \n(a):    Availability of urea in the year 2015- 16 and during the current year upto June, 2016 was \n329.00 LMT and 75.41 LMT against the requirement of 313.35 LMT and 67.00 LMT \nrespectively. There is no report of shortage of urea meant for agriculture and it is expected that \nconsequent to Neem Coati ng of Urea the diversion will reduce.   Moreover, Government has \ndeclared fertilizer as an essential commodity under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955 and \nnotified Fertilizer Control Order, 1985 and Fertilizer (Movement Control) Order, 1973.  The State \nGovernments have been empowered to take appropriate action to curb diversion of fertilizers \nand initiate action against the persons who violate these orders. Department of Fertilizers has, \nfrom time to time, written to the Chief Secretaries of various States and Ministry of Home Affairs \nto activate the enforcement agencies under their jurisdiction to take appropriate action to curb \ndiversion.   \n \n \n\n-: 2 :- \n (b)& (c):  Government of India through Notification No. 12012/1/2015- FPP dated 25.05.2015 \nhas made it mandatory for all domestic producers to produce 100 % Neem Coated Urea (NCU). \nEntire quantity of indigenously produced subsidized urea is being neem coated w. e.f. 1 st  \nSeptember, 2015.  Imported Urea is also being neem coated w.e.f. Ist December, 2015.Since \nNeem Coated Urea has been introduced recently, it is too early to assess its impact on subsidy \nand import. \n******** \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_449": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 449 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nPROJECTS FOR MODERNISATION OF POLICE \n \n449.   SHRI K. PARASURAMAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has  implemented specific projects to  \nmodernize police forces and Central Armed  Police Force (CAPF) in the \ncountry; \n \n(b) if so, the details of the said projects; \n \n(c) the  action taken by the Government  to establish necessary \ninfrastructure for police and CAPF personnel to enable them  to face new \nchallenges; and \n \n(d) the details of allocation of funds  made by the Government for this \npurpose during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HANSRAJ GANGARAM AHIR) \n \n(a) & (b):  The Government of India has been supplementing the efforts \nand resources of the States by implementing the Scheme for \nModernisation of Police Forces (MPF Scheme). Under this scheme, funds \nare approved and released to the State Governments for plan \ncomponents, such as police operational buildings and housing, and non -\nplan components, such as weapons, equipment for training,  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 449 FOR 19.07.2016 \ncomputerization, forensic science, Mega City Policing (MCP), mobility. \n“Police” being state  subject as per the Seventh Schedule of the \nConstitution, the projects are identified, formulated and implemented by \nthe State Governments based on their actual requirements. However, so \nfar as Central Armed Police Forces are concerned, Modernisation Plan – \nII is being implemented to increase the strike capability of Forces with \nsuperior arms & ammunitions, strategic equipment, effective transport \nvehicles, state -of-the-art communication, surveillance and border \nguarding systems which are the mainstay of any operational strategy.  \n \n(c)& (d) For establishing and upgrading of infrastructure of State \nPolice organizations, under the MPF scheme, the Government has \nallocated and released funds during the last three years and the current \nyear to the State Governments as below: \n(₹ in crore) \n \n \n \n...3/- \n \nYear Allocation Released Plan Non-Plan  Total  \n2013-14 1097.00 245.62 1342.62 1338.35 \n2014-15 860.00 537.50 1397.50 1397.22 \n2015-16 NIL 595.00 595.00 595.00 \n2016-17 NIL 595.00 595.00 NIL* \n\n-3- \n \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 449 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \n* Presently Annual Action Plans of the States are being considered and \non approval of these Plans, releasing of funds will be commenced. \n \nAn allocation of Rs.432.90 crore  has been approved for Mega City \nPolicing in six cities, namely, Hyderabad, Chennai, Mumbai, Bengaluru, \nKolkata and Ahmedabad and till date funds amounting to Rs.184.52 crore \nhas been released. Under this component, projects of CCTV surveillance, \nCommand C ontrol Centre (CCC),  Command Control Centre (CCC), Dial \n100 system, Fusion Centre/Data Centre,   Highway Patrol Cars and Aerial \nSurveillance (UAV/Helicopters) are being implemented by the respective  \nState Governments in these megacities. \n As regards CAPFs, details of allocation of funds for the last three years \nin respect of Modernisation Plan are as below: \n \n \n               Year  \nCAPF \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \nAssam Rifles 33.37 23.41 31.88 \nBSF 38.31 42.83 59.64 \nCISF 5.55 8.93 10.23 \nCRPF 1.86 5.00 80.54 \nITBP 5.50 5.32 21.00 \nNSG 5.50 5.94 5.94 \nSSB 8.98 5.50 14.00 \nTotal  99.07 96.93 223.23 \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_450": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 450 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nSECURITY TO PRIVATE SECTOR \n \n450.   DR. P. VENUGOPAL: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Central Industrial  Security Force (CISF) has decided to  \nprovide full time security cover by its armed  commandos to the private \nsector establishment; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether it is a fact that the CISF has  been receiving payment from \nthese private  sector establishments for the security  provided by CISF \ncommandos; and \n \n(d) if so, the total amount received from  each establishment along with \nthe strength  of personnel deployed by CISF in each  unit of the private \nsector establishments  during each of the last three years and the  \ncurrent year, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n \n(a) & (b)  The CISF Act, 1968 as amended vide Act No.22 of 2009, \nprovides for deployment of CISF in private units also.  As on date, CISF is \ndeployed in 08 Private Sector Units, as detailed below:- \n \n \n...2/- \n\n-2- \n \nLS.U.S.Q.NO. 450 FOR 19.07.2016 \n \n \n(c) & (d)  The cost of CISF deployment at respectiv e private sector \nestablishments is borne by the private establishment concerned.  The \ndetails are given below:- \nAmount in Rs \n   2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 GRAND \nTOTAL \nSN\nO \nNAME OF \nPRIVATE \n ESTT. \nSANCTIONED \nSTRENGTH \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \nPAYMENT \nRECEIVED \n KARNATAKA       \n1 Infosys Tech  \nBangalore \n176 41769759 49929409 54084208 12771957 158555333 \n2 Electronic City  \nBangalore \n84 27809608 31982638 32603426 8037192 100432864 \n3 Infosys Tech  \nMysore \n105 18567578 22532267 24865527 6049940 72015312 \n GUJARAT       \n4 Reliance  \nIndustries  \n217 90257442 99756021 105316944 25805927 321136334 \n5 CGPL/ UMPL \n Mundra \n99 0 0 24462167 12888549 37350716 \n MAHARASHTRA       \n6 Infosys Tech  \nPune \n134 61654991 69034992 74724780 18044112 223458875 \n ODISHA       \n7 Tata Steel Ltd.  \nKalingnagar \n278 0 0 23819076 23819076 47638152 \n UTTARAKHAND       \n8 Patanjali Food & \nHarbalPark \nHaridwar \n35 0 0 11688240 3891273 15579513 \n  TOTAL 1128 240059378 273235327 351564368 111308026 976167099 \n \n***** \nSNo Pvt Sector Unit Strength sanctioned for deployment \n1 Infosys Tech Bangalore, Karnataka 176 \n2 Electronic City Bangalore, Karnataka 84 \n3 Infosys Tech Mysore, Karnataka 105 \n4 Reliance Industries, Gujarat 217 \n5 CGPL/UMPL  Mundra, Gujarat 99 \n6 Infosys Tech Pune, Mahrashtra 134 \n7 Tata Steel Ltd. Kalingnagar, Odisha 278 \n8 Patanjali Food & HerbalPark Haridwar, \nUttarakhand \n35 ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_451": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 451 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2016 \n \nRENTED GODOWNS \n \n451.  SHRI AJAY MISRA TENI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h   be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the available storage capacity of FCI against the procurement  \ntarget of rice and wheat in Uttar Pradesh is low; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof indicating the total covered and open storage \ncapacity available in the State; \n \n(c)  whether despite the utilization of  storage capacity available in the State \nabout 30 lakh tonnes of foodgrains are kept in the rented godowns and most of \nthese godowns are owned by trading mafia who misuse these godowns to deny \nthe benefits of the Government schemes to the bonafide beneficiaries; \n \n(d)  if so, whether the Government  proposes to construct new godowns as per  \nits procurement needs; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details of the action plan finalised for the purpose? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a)& (b): No, Madam. Sufficient storage capacity is available in Uttar Pradesh (UP) \nregion with Food Corporation of India (FCI) and state agencies for meeting \nprocurement targets.  The total  capacity of  covered & open storage in the form \nof Cover and Plinth (CAP) facilities  currently available in UP with various \nagencies as on 30.06.2016 is as under :- \nStorage Agency Covered Capacity  \n(in Lakh MT) \nCAP Capacity \n(in Lakh MT) \nTOTAL  \n(in Lakh MT) \nFCI 14.95 5.19 20.14 \nCWC # 8.20 0 8.20 \nSWC $ 19.44 0.65 20.09 \nPEG * 12.53 0 12.29 \nTotal 55.12 5.84 60.96 \n# Central Warehousing Corporation.  \n$ State Warehousing Corporation. \n* Constructed and hired under Private Entrepreneurs Guarantee (PEG) Scheme.  \n(c), (d) & (e): In view of reply to (a) & (b) given above, question does not arise. \n**** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_453": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 453 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nGM Crops \n \n 453. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) the details of Genetically Modified (GM) crops/seeds approved for field trials in the \ncountry in the recent past; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to grant permission for re -trial of 15-GM seeds as per \nthe new norms; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government proposes to start a nation- wide discussion again by forming an \nexpert committee to assess the advantages and disadvantages of genetically modified \ncrops in view of the rising demands of foodgrains; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the names of GM crops which are permitted for \ncultivation and the outcome thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, \nFOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a) In India, the permission for conduct of confined field trials of GM crops (also referred as \nGenetically Engineered (GE) crops are approved by the Genetic Engineering Appraisal \nCommittee (GEAC) as per “Rules for the manufacture, use, import, export & storage of \nhazardous micro organisms, genetically engineered organisms or cells, 1989” notified \nunder the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986. GM cotton, corn, brinjal, maize, potato, \nrice & chickpea with insect resistance or herbicide tolerance or both have been approved \nfor conduct of confined field trials in the country in the last three years. \n \n(b) & (c) The permission for conduct of confined field trials of GM crops is given on a case \nby case basis after extensive review of the application by GEAC. There is neither a \nproposal nor any new norms to grant permission for re-trial of 15 GM seeds.  \n \n\n(d) GEAC is the apex committee consisting of representatives from the concerned \nministries/departments and experts for regulation of GM crops on a case by case basis as \nper “Rules for the manufacture, use, import, export & storage of hazardous micro \norganisms, genetically engineered organisms or cells, 1989” notified under the \nEnvironment (Protection) Act, 1986. The advantages/disadvantages of GM c rops are \nreviewed on a case by case basis as part of evaluation process by GEAC and is ongoing \nprocess.  \n \n(e) As on date, Bt cotton is the only crop approved for commercial cultivation in India. Bt \ncotton hybrids containing five approved events are widely cultivated in India.   \n \n \n************** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_454": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 454 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nVISA POLICY FOR BRICS COUNTRIES \n \n454.   SHRI G. HARI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is  considering for a visa waiver/visa on \narrival for business visitors and tourists belonging  to Brazil, Russia, \nIndia, China and South Africa (BRICS) countries; and \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n(a) & (b): A proposal in this regard received from the Department of \nCommerce is under consideration.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_455": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 455 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2016/ ASHADHA 28, 1938 (SAKA) \n \nWithdrawal of AFSPA from NER \n \n455.   SHRIMATI VANAROJA R.: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is  considering to withdraw the Armed \nForces Special Powers Act (AFSPA) in the North Eastern region; \n \n(b) whether it is true that the North East States have been demanding its \nremoval for a long time and if so, the reaction of the Government \nthereto; \n \n(c) whether the said Act has been  removed in some parts of the North \nEast; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI KIREN RIJIJU) \n \n \n(a):  No, Madam. At present there is no proposal under consideration to \nwithdraw AFSPA from the North Eastern Region. \n \n(b):  The notification for extension of notification under the Act is \nissued with the concurrence of the state governments. Govts of \nArunachal Pradesh, Assam , Meghalaya have been recommending \nissuance of notifications in these states/portions of states. Government  \n...2/- \n\n-2- \nLS.U.S.Q.No. 455 FOR 19.07.2016 \nof Nagaland has not supported issuance of notificatio n in that state. For \nstate of M anipur, notification of AFSPA is issued by the state \ngovernment.  \n \n(c) & (d): AFSPA, 1958 was withdrawn form the areas falling in the \njurisdiction of 30 Police Stations of Tripu ra by State Government vide its \nNotifications dated 27.5.2015. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_456": "O.I.H.\n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n456\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nINTER\n-\nSTATE TRADE OF \nPOTATO\n \n \n456. \n \nSHRI SUNIL KUMAR SINGH: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe Government proposes to formulate any concrete policy to promote \nthe inter\n-\nstate trade of agricultural produce including potato; \n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe per annum quantum of potato in tonne procured by Jharkhand from other \nStates;\n \n \n \n(d) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to implement any special scheme to promote \nthe cultivation of potato in various States of the country including, Jharkhand; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nif so, the details thereof along with the financial assistance provided by the \nGo\nvernment to various States in this regard?\n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n&\n \n(b)\n:\n   \n \nThe Government implements Central Sector Scheme namely National \nAgricultural Market (e\n-\nNAM), whose objective \ninter alia\n \nis to develop barrier free \nNational market to promote inter\n-\nstate trade of agricultural produce including potato. \n \n \n(c) to (e):\n \nAs per i\nnformation provided by Government of Jharkhand, the annual \nreported quantum of arrival of potato in Jharkhand from other States is approximately \n2,78,543 tonne. Presently there is no special scheme to promote the cultivation of \npotato in the various States\n \nof the country including Jharkhand.\n \n\n \n********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_457": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n457\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nWOMEN FRIENDLY AGRICULTURAL EQUIPMENTS \n \n      \n \n457. \n \nSHRI VINAYAK BHAURAO RAUT: \n \nSHRI ANANDRAO ADSUL: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nmigration of agricultural workers and poor people from rural areas to \ncities in search of jobs has adversely impacted the agriculture/farming sector in the \ncountry;\n \n \n(b) \n \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government is considering a plan to d\nevelop women\n-\nfriendly farm \nequipment to sustain the agriculture sector; \n \n \n(d) \n \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe action being taken by the Government in this regard?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFAR\nE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n(a)\n \n& (b)\n:\n \nAs per Economic Survey 2013\n-\n14, the share of agriculture and allied \nsector in total employment in the country declined from 59.9% in 1999\n-\n2000 to \n58.5% in \n2004\n-\n05 and further to 48.9% in 2011\n-\n12. This reduction is on account of growth of \nsecondary and tertiary sector and is considered as a natural part\n \nof the development \nprocess. Main reasons for migration include poverty, high population pressure o\nn land, \nlack of basic infrastructural facilities like health care and education, better job \nopportunities, relatively higher wages etc. However, the adverse impact of migration on \nthe agriculture/farming sector is compensated by increased level of agricult\nural \nmechanization which is evident \nfrom the fact that the farm power availability in India has \nincreased from 0.47 kW/ha in the year 2000 to 1.84 kW/ha in the year 2013\n-\n14.\n \n   \n \n\n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(c) to (e):\n \nIn order to enable women to perform various farm \noperations with lesser \ndrudgery, various implements and \nhand tools have been developed by different \nagricultural institutes under In\ndian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) viz. Central \nInstitute for Women in Agriculture, Bhubaneswar; Central Institute\n \nof Agricultural \nEngineering (C\nI\nEAE), Bhopal and All India Coordinated Research Project (AICRP) \nCentres in State Agriculture Universities. The National Gender Resource Centre in \nAgriculture \nunder the Department of Agriculture, Cooperation & Farmers Welfare\n \nhas \nbrought out a compendium of the Gender Friendly Tools/Equipments for Women in \nAgriculture.\n \n \n \nThe use of gender friendly equipments\n \nis promoted through training, \ndemonstration and financial assistance under Sub\n-\nMission on Agricultural \nMechanization. Wo\nmen beneficiaries are provided 10% additional financial assistance \nfor purchase of various agricultural machines and equipments. State Governments have \nalso been directed to ensure flow of benefits to women farmers by way of earmarking at \nleast 30% of allo\ncation of this Sub\n-\nMission.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n                                                                                                    \n \n \n \n**********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_458": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n458\n                       \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE  \n1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2016\n \n \nMANGO PRODUCTION \n \n \n458. \n \nSHRI RABINDRA KUMAR JENA: \n \n      \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n‡ãðŠãäÓã \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(a) \n \nwhether\n \nthe mango production and export is likely to drop due to unseasonal \nrainfall this year, if so, the details thereof indicating the production of mango, State\n-\nwise \nalong with its export during each of the last three years and the expected foreign \nexchange e\narnings from the surplus mango production during the current year; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government is considering to provide assistance to the affected \nfarmers and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n \n(c) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to promote mango prod\nuction in the country?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF  STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF  AGRICULTURE AND  FARMERS  WELFARE\n \n \n‡ãðŠãäÓã  \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããÊã¾ã ½ãñâ Àã•¾ã ½ãâ¨ããè          \n \n( SHRI\n \nSUDARSHAN BHAGAT\n)  \n \n \n \n(a)\n:\n   \n \nAccording to the information received from \nthe states so far, there is likely to be \nan increase of 6% in mango production in the year, 2015\n-\n16 as compared to the \nprevious year, 2014\n-\n15. However, there has been drop in the mango exports during the \nyear 2015\n-\n16.\n \n \n \nThe details of state wise production\n \nof mango during the last three years and the \ncurrent year and Exports with Foreign Exchange\n \nEarnings are at Table 1 and 2 of \nAnnexure\n-\nI respectively.\n \n \n(b):\n \nIn the states like Telangana, which has reported that there might be some mango \nfarmers affected du\ne to weather vagaries, \n \nbased on the final enumeration reports, the \nassistance will be provided to the affected farmers as per State Disaster Response \nFunds (SDRF) norms.\n \n \n\nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n-\n2\n-\n \n \n \n(c):\n \nSeveral steps taken by the Government to increase the Country’\ns production of \nfruits and vegetables including mango are as under:\n \n \n\n \nMission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH), a Centrally \nSponsored Scheme is being implemented during XII Plan w.e.f. 2014\n-\n15, for \nholistic growth of the horticulture sector\n \ncovering all the horticulture crops \nincluding fruits and vegetables.\n \n \n\n \nThe Mission envisages production and productivity improvement of horticulture \ncrops including fruit and vegetable crops through increased coverage of area \nwith improved cultiva\nrs, rejuv\nenation of senile \n \norchards, protected cultivation, \ncreation of water resources, adoption of Integrated Pest Management (IPM), \nIntegrated Nutrient Management (INM), organic farming, including insitu \ngeneration of organic inputs. Capacity buildings of farme\nrs and technicians is \nalso provided for adopting improved technologies.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \nTable\n-\n1: \nStatewise Production of Mango\n \nP: Production in '000 Tonnes\n \nSTATES/UTs \n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n2015\n-\n16 \n \n(2nd Adv. Est)\n \nANDAMAN NICOBAR\n \n2.80\n \n3.85\n \n2.93\n \n2.50\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n4406.92\n \n2737.01\n \n2822.08\n \n3049.93\n \nASSAM\n \n55.27\n \n44.59\n \n45.69\n \n57.86\n \nBIHAR\n \n1363.80\n \n1367.57\n \n1272.00\n \n1464.90\n \nCHHATISGARH\n \n291.83\n \n327.91\n \n386.67\n \n321.75\n \nDAMAN & DIU\n \n0.00\n \n0.00\n \n2.05\n \n0.00\n \nDELHI\n \n0.00\n \n0.00\n \n0.15\n \n0.00\n \nGOA\n \n9.04\n \n8.94\n \n8.89\n \n2.72\n \nGUJARAT\n \n1003.71\n \n1125.61\n \n1219.71\n \n1301.00\n \nHARYANA\n \n79.55\n \n86.61\n \n88.72\n \n118.20\n \nHIMACHAL PRADESH\n \n50.00\n \n25.41\n \n47.61\n \n38.34\n \nJAMMU & KASHMIR\n \n23.09\n \n6.99\n \n20.93\n \n29.41\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n517.92\n \n517.92\n \n523.14\n \n414.39\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n1795.10\n \n1755.56\n \n1646.51\n \n1652.50\n \nKERALA\n \n441.03\n \n441.03\n \n252.86\n \n386.38\n \nMADHYA PRADESH\n \n376.00\n \n379.73\n \n396.00\n \n436.01\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \n633.00\n \n1212.50\n \n758.84\n \n522.87\n \nMIZORAM\n \n3.47\n \n3.68\n \n4.18\n \n4.18\n \nNAGALAND\n \n3.00\n \n3.57\n \n3.57\n \n4.05\n \nODISHA\n \n753.79\n \n751.02\n \n769.93\n \n723.13\n \nPUDUCHERRY\n \n3.41\n \n2.57\n \n3.57\n \n3.24\n \nPUNJAB\n \n105.92\n \n107.57\n \n113.44\n \n119.40\n \nRAJASTHAN \n \n70.17\n \n79.90\n \n78.68\n \n72.53\n \nTAMIL NADU\n \n714.08\n \n785.50\n \n896.78\n \n1660.00\n \nTELANGANA\n \n \n \n1717.88\n \n1801.75\n \n1797.80\n \nTRIPURA\n \n28.85\n \n56.28\n \n62.56\n \n56.06\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n4386.99\n \n4300.98\n \n4347.50\n \n4611.20\n \nUTTARAKHAND\n \n148.64\n \n150.44\n \n150.62\n \n150.67\n \nWEST BENGAL\n \n735.00\n \n430.71\n \n799.65\n \n693.39\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nTOTAL\n \n18002.38\n \n18431.33\n \n18526.98\n \n19694.41\n \nSource: Horticulture Statistics Division, DAC&FW\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nTable\n-\n2: Exports of Mango \nwith Foreign Exchange Earnings\n \nQty. In MT\n \nValue in \nUS $ Mill.\n \nMANGO EXPORTS\n \nYear\n \nQty\n \nValue\n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n2,03,400.68\n \n160.27\n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n2,16,140.30\n \n177.19\n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n1,97,818.99\n \n187.66\n \n2015\n-\n16\n \n1,65,195.02\n \n170.82\n \nSource: APEDA Website\n \nNote: Exports include Fresh/Dried Mango and Mango Pulp\n. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n**********\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_459": " \n \nOIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.459 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nCRZ Notification \n459.  SHRI GOPAL SHETTY: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Union Government has received any proposal from the State Government of \nMaharashtra for environmental clearance regarding issue of Greater Mumbai pertaining to \nCRZ Notification 2011 (MCGM), preparation of new development plan for Mumbai \nincluding redevelopment of dilapidated, cessed and unsafe buildings;  \n(b) if so, the details in this regard;  \n(c) the present status of the said proposals; and  \n(d) the time by which the said proposals are likely to be finalised along with the reasons for \ndelay in this regard? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n \n(a) No proposal has been received in  the Ministry  from Government of Maharashtra seeking \nenvironmental clearance under Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) Notification, 2011 for new \ndevelopment plan of Mumbai including redevelopment of dilapidated, cessed  and unsafe \nbuildings in CRZ areas of Mumbai.   \n \n (b) to (d) : Does not arise in view of answer at (a) above.  \n \n*** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_460": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OE ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 460 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2016 \n \nSmuggling/Trade of Animals \n \n460. SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:  \n \nWill the Minster of ENVIRONMENT, FORESTS AND  CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether it is a fact that there are hundreds of websites involved in trade of rare animals \nand their body parts; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government is contemplating to bring out guidelines/legislation specifica lly \nto curb smuggling and online trading of animals and their body parts; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor and the other steps taken/proposed \nto be taken to prevent such illegal acts in the country? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE)  FOR ENVIRONMENT, FOREST \nAND CLIMATE CHANGE \n  \n(SHRI ANIL MADHAV DAVE) \n \n(a)  & (b)  Several websites are seen advertising sale of rare animals and their parts. \nThe details of various websites advertising rare animals and their parts \ncollated by Wildlife Crime Control Bureau are at Annexure A. \n( c )& (d)  Illegal online smuggling of rare animals and their parts are being \nmonitored under relevant laws existing under Department of Information \nTechnology at both State and Central Government as a part of combating \ncybercrime. \nFollowing steps are being taken by this Ministry through Wildlife Crime \nControl Bureau with limited resources available with it, to prevent such \nillegal acts: \n1. Services of Cyber Crime Specialist on Contractual b asis is being \nutilized to carry out regular cyber patrolling to detect any posts and \noffers over such trade portals on the World Wide Web: \n2. On detection of such offers, details are retrieved of the suspect and the \n\ninformation is passed on to relevant enforc ement agencies for legal \naction: \n3. A meeting of representatives from online trade portals was convened \non 19th May 2016  to discuss issues pertaining to online wildlife trade \nand sensitize them on illegal wildlife trade and to discuss modalities to \nassist Wildlife Crime Control Bureau in case of such detection. \n4. During training and sensitization programme conducted by the \nBureau, the issue of illegal online wildlife trade is being highlighted so \nthat the officials involved in enforcement are abreast of such tr ends \nand taken necessary action. \n**** \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-A \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO . 460 REGARDING “SMUGGLING/TRADE OF \nANIMALS”  BY SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN  FOR REPLY ON 19.07.2016 \n \nLIST OF WEBSITES INVOLVED IN TRADE OF RARE ANIMALS AND THEIR BODY \nPARTS , AS HAS BEEN NOTICED TILL DATE BY WILDLIFE CRIME CONTROL \nBUREAU \n1. Quikr.com  21. Aliexpress.com \n2. Gumtree.com  22. ocala4sale.com \n3. ClickIndia.in  23. claz.org \n4. Olx.in  24. Meramall.com \n5. eBay.com 25. Indiagrid.com \n6. Adsglobe.com 26. salespider.com \n7. Pets.oodle.com 27. domesticsale.com \n8. Sell.com 28. Adsmantra.com \n9. Click.in 29 epage.com \n10. Classifiedads.com 30. recycler.com \n11. Indialist.com 31. classifiedclan.com \n12. Locanto.in 32. hoobly.com \n13. globalFreeClassifiedAds.com 33. freeadlists.com \n14. Adpost.com 34. adpost.com \n15. Alibaba.com 35. trade.indiamart.com \n16. Webindia123.com 36. usfreeads.com \n17. intgiant.com 37. usnetads.com \n18. Yakaz.com 48. Theturtlesource.com \n19. Pennysaveruse.com 39. parrotshandraised.com.au \n20. daype.com 40. backwaterreptiles.com \n\n41. Adeex.com 67. youtube.com \n42. Whereincity.com 68. reptilesncritters.com \n43. myaquariumclub.com 69. Highlandclassifieds \n44. citybase.in 70. Ozfreeonline.com \n45. Ricepulier.com 71. Kugli.com \n46. Amazon.com 72. Marketplace.sfgate.com \n47. Buytigers.com 73. Ablewise.com \n48. Softbillsforsale.com 74. Classifiedsforfree.com \n49. Bole.com 75. Oraigslist.org \n50. Shopping.rediff.com 76. Domesticsale.com \n51. Vastustore.com 77. Freeadlists.com \n52. www.einiabusiness.com 78. Geebo.com \n53. www.netexpress.co.in 79. Hot-ads.com \n54. www.whereincity.com 80. Jihoy.com \n55. Beatyourprice.com 81. Kaango.com \n56. Taxidemiemporium.com 82. Nareshgroup.com \n57. Boostime.in 83. Loot.com \n58. Bizmartindia.com 84. Pennysaveruss.com \n59. Worldslist.com 85. Snakesvenom.com \n60. Postaroo.com 86. Paintbrushes.com \n61. Freeadvertisingexchange.com 87. Ashbrush.com \n62. Freeadscity.com 88. Startortoiseuk.co.uk \n63. Rajb2b.com 89. Tortoisesupply.com \n64. Bavun.in 90. Turtlesale.com \n65. Wantedwants.com 91. Snakesalsunset.com \n66. Dragg.in 92. Market.kingsnake.com \n\n93. Buysellcommunity.com 100. Petsmart.com \n94. Bharatpatal 101 Petsolutions.com \n95. Reptilecity.com 102 Undergroundreptiles.com \n96. Firstchoicereptiles.com 103. Faunaclassifieds.com \n97. Comsnake.net 104. Havocscope.com \n98 Hookbillsforwsale.com 105. Trantraveda.org \n99. Clickbd.com 106. Snapdeal.com \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_461": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 461 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nINTRODUCTION OF RAJDHANI TRAIN \n \n†461. SHRI CHANDRAKANT KHAIRE: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has not introduced any superfast or \nRajdhani train between Delhi and Aurangabad for the facility of \ntourists coming from various parts of the world keeping in view the \nworld famous Ajanta caves situated at Aurangabad;  \n \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; and  \n \n(c) the reaction of the Government thereto? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n (a) to (c)  Aurangabad station is presently connected to New Delhi \nby one pair of superfast train viz. 12715/12716 Amritsar -H.S. Nanded \nExpress (daily).  Introduction of new superfast train from \nAurangabad to New Delhi is not feasible, at present, due to \noperational and resource constraints.   \n            Rajdhani Express trains are long distance fast moving \nprestigious trains primarily meant for connecting National capital \nand State capitals, running on very tight timings and with limited \nhalts enroute.   As such, at present there is no proposal to introduce \nRajdhani Express train from New Delhi to Aurangabad. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_462": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 462 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nIndependent Channel \n \n462.   SHRI B.N. CHANDRAPPA: \n           \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to create an independent \nchannel without any advertisements during the telecast of cricket \nmatches and if so, the details thereof; \n  \n(b) whether the Government has taken steps to consult various \nstakeholders in this regard; and \n \n(c) if so, the time by which the independent channel is likely to be \nintroduced? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR YOUTH AFFAIRS \nAND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJEY GOEL) \n \n \n(a)  Madam, Ministry of Information & Broadcasting has informed that \nthere  is no such proposal to create an independent channel without any  \nadvertisements during the telecast of cricket matches. \n(b)&(c)  Does not arise.  \n \n*****\n\n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_463": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINORITY AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †463 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nDEMOGRAPHIC PROFILE OF MINORITIES \n \n†463. SHRI  VISHNU  DAYAL  RAM: \nSHRI  RAM  CHARITRA  NISHAD: \n \nWill the Minister of MINORITY AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has conducted any survey regarding \ndemographic profile of minorities in the country during the last three years \nand the current year; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the number of such surveys conducted by the \nprevious Governments and the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Government has any proposal to increase funds to be provided \nfor development of minorities on priority basis in the districts having \nminority population; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINORITY AFFAIRS (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI MUKHTAR ABBAS NAQVI) \n \n(a) & (b): No, Madam. \n \n(c) & (d): As against the Budget provision of Rs. 3,712.78 crore provided during \n2015-16, Rs. 3,6 32.26 Crore (provisional data) was expended by the Minis try of \nMinority Affairs for the welfare of minorities. For the year 2016 -17, t he \nGovernment has increased the Budget provision for the Ministry at Rs. 3,800.00 \ncrore.  \n \n \n* * * * * ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_464": " \n  \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.464  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20 TH  JULY, 2016 \n \nTOWERS IN RESIDENTIAL AREAS \n \n464.  SHRI PONGULETI SRINIVASA REDDY:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the National Human Rights Commission h as issued notice to the \nGovernment in the wake of allegations in a complaint that mobile towers in the vicinity \nof residential areas, emit radiations that are hazardous to human health; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  the strategy/measures the Government is adopti ng/planning to adopt to ensure \nthat no substantive damage is caused to the life an d health of human and other living \nbeings; \n \n(d)  whether as per Government order dated 21.08.20 13, the local selfcGovernment \ninstitutions should confirm that the proposed tower s do not come within 500 m of \nschools/hospitals, but it does not cover residentia l areas and due to this order mobile \noperators are violating the safe radiation range an d are erecting towers in thickly \npopulated residential areas; and \n \n(e)  if so, the details thereof and the measures ta ken by the Government to \naddress the problem? \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (IC) OF THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS & \n MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI MANOJ SINHA) \n \n(a) & (b)  Yes, Madam. Law Division of National Hum an Rights Commission \n(NHRC) has issued a notice dated 02c06c2016 in case no. 233/90/0/2016 to Ministry \nof Communication and Information Technology in resp ect of a complaint regarding \nimpact of EMF emission from mobile towers on health . A suitable reply to the notice \nhas been submitted to the Commission inter alia bringing out the following:  \n \n “With regard to impact of Electromagnetic Field (E MF) emissions from mobile \ntowers on health, World Health Organization (WHO) h as referred to approximately \n25,000 articles published around the world over pas t 30 years, and based on an inc\ndepth review of scientific literature, has stated, “ Despite the feeling of some people \nthat more research needs to be done, scientific knowledge in this area is now more \nextensive than for most chemicals.” WHO has concluded c “current evidence does not \nconfirm the existence of any health consequences from exposure to low level \nelectromagnetic fields ”.   \n \nWHO in its Fact Sheet No. 304, May 2006 on Electrom agnetic Fields and \nPublic Health (Base Stations and Wireless Technolog ies) has concluded that “ From \nall evidence accumulated so far, no adverse short or long term health effects have \nbeen shown to occur from the RF Signals produced by base stations kelipsis.. considering \nthe very low exposure levels and research results collected to date, there is no \nconvincing scientific evidence that the weak RF Signals from base stations and \nwireless networks caused adverse health effects. ” Also, in September 2013, WHO in \nonline question and answers have mentioned that \" Studies to date provide no \nindication that environmental exposure to RF fields, such as from base stations, \nincreases the risk of cancer or any other disease.\"   \n \nContdkelipsis2/c \n\n \n  \n \n \n \nc2c \n \nFurther, several High Courts viz Hon’ble High Court s of Punjab & Haryana, \nMadras, Kerala, Gujarat, Delhi, Himachal Pradesh an d Allahabad have dismissed \npetitions, where the mobile tower installations wer e challenged on grounds of health \neffects of EMF radiations at various locations including residential. \n \n(c)  Department of Telecommunications (DoT) have be en monitoring global \ndevelopments and has already taken necessary steps and adopted stricter norms for \nsafety from EMF emmissions from mobile towers. Gove rnment of India has been \ntaking due precautions and necessary actions in res pect of EMF radiation emitted \nfrom mobile towers through various guidelines & nor ms issued from time to time \ntaking into account the international standards/nor ms. Presently the norms for \nexposure limit for the Radio Frequency Field (Base Station Emissions) in India are \nalready 10 times more stringent than the safe limit s prescribed by International \nCommission on Non Ionizing Radiation Protection (IC NIRP) and recommended by \nWHO. Further, Government of India has taken adequat e steps to ensure that \nTelecommunications Service Providers strictly adhere to these prescribed norms. \n  \nAll new mobile Base Transmitting Stations (BTS) sit es start radiating \ncommercially only after ensuring adherence to prescribed norms and after submission \nof self certificate to this effect to relevant Tele com Enforcement Resource & \nMonitoring (TERM) field offices of DoT. In order to  ensure compliance to the \nprescribed stricter precautionary norms of EMF radi ation from mobile tower, the \nextensive audit of compliance of selfccertificates being submitted by telecom service \nproviders and Base Transceiver Station (BTS) sites is carried out by TERM field units \nof  DoT. In case, any BTS site is found to violate the prescribed EMF norms, actions \nare taken to put a penalty of Rs. 10 lakh per BTS p er incidence including closing of \nBTS site, if the violation persists. Additionally, the BTS sites against which there are \npublic complaints are also tested by TERM Cell.  \n \n(d) & (e) The main conclusion from the WHO reviews is that EMF exposures \nbelow the limits recommended in the International C ommission on Non Ionizing \nRadiation Protection (ICNIRP) international guideli nes do not appear to have any \nknown consequence on health. In India norms for exp osure limit for the Radio \nFrequency Field (Base Station Emissions) are alread y 10 times more stringent than \nthe safe limits prescribed by ICNIRP and recommende d by WHO. Making the norms \nten times stricter than what has been prescribed by  ICNIRP and recommended by \nWHO, obviates the need for having separate norms fo r special localities like schools, \nhospitals and residential areas. Further, Governmen t of India has taken adequate \nsteps to ensure that Telecommunications Service Pro viders strictly adhere to these \nprescribed norms. \n \nAccordingly, the advisory guidelines issued by DoT to state governments on \n01.08.2013 for issue of clearance for installation of mobile towers do not place any \nrestriction on installation of mobile towers in pub lic buildings/places such as \nresidential areas, schools, hospitals, playground etc. \n \n \n*********************** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_465": " \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.465 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nBIO-TOILETS  \n \n465. SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH: \n         SHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI: \n   \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS  be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of trains which have eco-friendly bio-toilet facility;  \n(b) whether any target has been set by the Government to provide \ngreen toilets in all trains and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; and \n(c) the  details of  funds that has been allocated for the above \npurpose in fiscal year, 2016-17? \n  \n ANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nOF THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \n( SHRI MANOJ SINHA ) \n \n(a)  to  (c)   A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n***** \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 465 BY SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH AND \nSHRI SUMEDHANAND SARSWATI  TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA \nON 20.07.2016  REGARDING BIO-TOILETS \n  \n \n(a) Bio-toilets, based on the indigenous design and technology \ndeveloped in association with Defence Research & Development \nOrganisation, are being proliferated in coaches by Indian Railways \nand there are about 800 trains which have eco -friendly bio -toilet \nfacility. Some of these trains, in which bio -toilets have been \nintroduced on more than 50% of coaches, are indicated below: \nRailway Trains \nCENTRAL  12140/12139 Sewagram Express, 11015/11016 \nKushi Nagar Express, 12105/12106 Vidhrabha \nExpress \nEAST \nCOAST  \n22871/22872 Bhubaneswar -Tirupati Express, \n12830/12829 Bhubaneswar -Chennai Express, \n12843/12844 Puri -Ahmedabad Express, \n18507/18508 Hirakud Express, 18518/18517 \nVisakhapatnam-Korba Express \nEAST \nCENTRAL  \n15227/15228 Muzaffarpur -Yesvantpur Express, \n12567/12568 Rajya Rani Express, 13301/13302 \nDhanbad-Tatanagar Suvarna Rekha Express \nEASTERN  12345/12346 Saraighat Express, 12311/12312 Kalka \nMail, 12307/12308 Jodhpur Express, 12343/12344 \nSealdah-Darjeeling Mail, 12377/12378 Padatik \nExpress  \nNORTH \nCENTRAL  \n12403/12404 Allahabad -Jaipur Express, \n12451/12452 Shram Shakti Express, 11107/11108 \nBundelkhand Express, 11125/11126 Gwalior -Indore \nExpress \nNORTH \nEASTERN  \n12581/12582 Manduadih -New Delhi Express, \n12533/12534 Pushpak Express, 12555/12556 \nGorakhdham Express, 15007/15008 Krishak \nExpress               \n    \n  \n-2/- \n\n \n-2- \n \nNORTHEAST \nFRONTIER  \n15648/15647 Guwahati-Lokmanya Tilak (T) Express, \n15618/15617 Naharlagun -Guwahati Intercity \nExpress, 22511/22512 Lokmanya Tilak (T) -Kamkhya \nKarambhoomi Express \nNORTHERN  12429/12430 Lucknow -New Delhi AC Express, \n22418/22417 Mahamana Express, 14215/14216 \nGanga Gomti Express, 14008/14007 Sadbhavana \nExpress \nNORTH \nWESTERN  \n12458/12457 Bikaner -Delhi Sarai Rohilla Express,   \n12968/12967 Jaipur -Chennai Express, 12976/1 2975 \nJaipur-Mysuru Express, 12996/12995 Udaipur City -\nBandra (T) Express \nSOUTH \nCENTRAL  \n17016/17015 Visakha Express, 17018/17017 Rajkot \nExpress, 12702/12701 Hussain Sagar Express, \n17652/17651 Chennai Egmore -Kacheguda Express, \n17037/17038 Bikaner Express \nSOUTH \nEAST \nCENTRAL  \n \n18239-12855 Bilaspur -Nagpur Shivnath Express, \n58806 Gondia Passenger \nSOUTH \nEASTERN  \n12837/12838 Howrah -Puri Express,  12863/12864 \nHowrah-Yesvantpur Express, 12810/12809 Howrah -\nMumbai Mail   \nSOUTHERN  12637/12638 Pandiyan Express, 16101/16102 \nChennai Egmore -Rameswaram Express, \n16603/16604 Maveli Express, 16714/16713 \nRameswaram-Chennai Egmore Express, \n16723/16724 Ananthapuri Express, 16733/16734 \nRameswaram-Okha Express, 16780/16779 \nRameswaram-Tirupati Express \nSOUTH \nWESTERN  \n12627/12628 Karnataka Express,  12657/12658 \nBangalore Chennai Mail, 16525/16526 Bangalore - \nKanyakumari Express, 12253/12254 Yesv antpur-\nBhagalpur Anga Express \n.  \n.  \n -3/- \n\n \n-3- \nWEST \nCENTRAL  \n22981/22982 Kota -Sri Ganga Nagar Express, \n12121/12122 Madhya Pradesh Sampark Kranti \nExpress, 12191/12192 Shridham Express, \n12155/12156 Shane-E-Bhopal Express, 11449/11450 \nJabalpur-Sri Mata Vaishno Devi Katra Express  \nWESTERN  19005/19006 Mumbai Central -Okha Saurashtra \nExpress, 59547/59548 Okha Passenger, \n19216/19215 Mumbai Central -Porbandar Saurashtra \nExpress, 19269/19270 Porbandar -Muzaffarpur \nExpress \n \nIn addition to the above trains, coaches fitted with bio -toilets are \nrunning in other trains too in piecemeal manner. These trains are \nbeing progressively augmented with coaches equipped with bio -\ntoilets. \n \n(b)  Yes, Madam.  It  is planned to fit bio-toilets in the entire fleet of \nIndian Railways’ coaches by the year 2021-22. \n(c)  Funds of  ₹395 Crores have been allocated for the above \npurpose in fiscal year 2016-17. \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_466": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nDEPARTMENT OF ATOMIC ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 466 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nPRODUCTION OF TITANIUM METAL \n \n466.  SHRI  P. KUMAR: \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has any plan to start t he production of titanium metal \n and other value added materials from Rare Earths; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c)  the location where the Rare Earth Units are pr esently functioning in the \n country and the quantity of products produced from  these units? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR, PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVA NCES AND \nPENSIONS AND PRIME MINISTER'S OFFICE (DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n \n(a) No, Sir. \n \n(b) Does not arise in view of the reply at (a) abov e.  \n \n(c) The Location of Rare Earth Plant in India owned  by Indian Rare Earths \nLimited (IREL), a Public Sector Undertaking under t he Department of Atomic \nEnergy (DAE), is at Aluva, in Kerala.  The Plant, r efurbished for production of \nhigh pure rare earth, has been commissioned and com mercial production will \nstart during year 2016-17.   \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S9_U_467": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.467 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2016 \n \nBIOMETRIC DETAILS FOR VISA \n \n467.      SHRI RAJESHBHAI CHUDASAMA: \n     \nWill the Minister of EXTERNAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has rolled out a proje ct which ensures \ncollection of applicant’s biometric details before any kind of visa is \nissued; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof and whether out of the 178 Indian missions, \nonly 55 have been equipped with this biometric recording facility; \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether a proposal was floated to have the biometrics of the tourists \non arrival in India and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) whether the Government has taken a final call on enforcing  the \nbiometric aspect in the visa issuing process  and if so, the details \nthereof?  \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS \n[GEN. (DR) V. K. SINGH (RETD)] \n \n(a) to (e) The Ministry of Home Affairs has implemented a Mission Mode \nProject on Immigration, Visa and Foreigners Registration & Tracking \n(IVFRT). This Project is a multi -Ministry project which brings together a \nnumber of immigration, visa and registration tasks on to one IT platform, for \ngreater control and efficiency. The project also has provision for biometric \nenrolment and authentication. \n \nAs on date, out of our 178 Missions/Posts abroad, biometric enrolment \nsystem has been implemented in 79 Indian Missions/Posts. A list of these 79 \nIndian Missions/Posts abroad is attached as Annexure-I. \n \nThe Government of India launched the e-Tourist Visa (e-TV) facility on \nNovember 27, 2014 with a view to simplify the visa process for foreign \ntourists visiting India. The scheme has since been extended to 150 \ncountries. A list of such co untries is attached as Annexure -II. Biometric \ndetails of the foreigners visiting India on e-Tourist Visas (e-TV) are captured \non arrival at 16 designated Indian airports. \n***** \n\nANNEXURE-I \nBiometric Implemented Missions \n \nS.NO Country Name Mission Name S.NO Country Name Mission Name \n1 AFGANISTAN KANDHAR 41 NETHERLANDS THE HAGUE \n2 AFGANISTAN JALALABAD 42 NIGER NIAMEY \n3 AFGANISTAN HERAT 43 NIGERIA ABUJA \n4 AFGANISTAN KABUL 44 NIGERIA LAGOS \n5 AFGANISTAN MAZAR-E-SHARIEF 45 PALESTINE GAZA \nRAMALLAH \n6 ALGERIA ALGIERS 46 PERU LIMA \n7 ANGOLA LUANDA 47 PHILIPPINES MANILA \n8 AUSTRALIA PERTH 48 POLAND WARSAW \n9 AUSTRALIA CANBERRA 49 QATAR DOHA \n10 AUSTRALIA SYDNEY 50 REUNION ISLAND ST. DENIS \n11 AUSTRALIA MELBOURNE 51 ROMANIA BUCHAREST \n12 AZERBEIJAN BAKU 52 SAUDI ARABIA RIYADH \n13 BULGARIA SOFIA 53 SAUDI ARABIA JEDDAH \n14 CHILE SANTIAGO 54 SENEGAL DAKAR \n15 CHINA HONG KONG 55 SERBIA & \nMONTENEGRO \nBELGRADE \n16. CUBA HAVANA 56 SLOVAKIA BRATISLAVA \n17 DENMARK COPENHAGEN 57 SURINAME PARAMARIBO \n18 EGYPT CAIRO 58 SWEDEN STOCKHOLM \n19 GREECE ATHENS 59 SWITZERLAND GENEVA \n20 ICELAND REYKJAVIK 60 TANZANIA ZANZIBAR \n21 INDONESIA JAKARTA 61 TANZANIA DAR-ES-SALAAM \n22 INDONESIA MEDAN 62 THAILAND BANGKOK \n23 INDONESIA BALI 63 TRINIDAD & \nTOBAGO \nPORT OF SPAIN \n24 IRAN BANDAR ABBAS 64 TURKEY ANKARA \n25 IRAN TEHRAN 65 TURKEY ISTANBUL \n26 IRAN ZAHIDAN 66 TURKMENISTAN ASHGABAT \n27 JAMAICA KINGSTON 67 UK BIRMINGHAM \n28 JORDAN AMMAN 68 UK EDINBURGH \n29 KAZAKSTAN ALMATY 69 UK LONDON \n30 KOREA (SOUTH) SEOUL 70 UKRAINE KYIV \n31 KUWAIT KUWAIT 71 USA CHICAGO \n32 KYRGYSTAN BISHKEK 72 USA HOUSTON \n33 LATVIA RIGA 73 USA ATLANTA \n34 MADAGASCAR ANTANANARIVO 74 UZBEKISTAN TASHKENT \n35 MALAYSIA KUALA LUMPUR 75 VENEZUELA CARACAS \n36 MAURITIUS PORT LOUIS 76 YEMEN SANA A \n37 MONGOLIA ULAAN BAATAR 77 ARMENIA YEREVAN \n38 MYANMAR MANDALAY 78 ZAMBIA LUSAKA \n39 MYANMAR YANGON 79 ZIMBABWE HARARE \n40 NAMIBIA WINDHOEK    \n \n***** \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nList of countries extended e-Tourist Visa Scheme \n01 Andorra 51 Laos 101  Thailand \n02 An